consiSt("mi",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Michigan Supreme Court","d":"Court of last resort. Writes the Michigan Court Rules, regulates bar admission (through the Board of Law Examiners) and lawyer discipline (through the Attorney Grievance Commission and Attorney Discipline Board). Hears civil cases almost entirely by discretionary application for leave to appeal, including ‘bypass’ applications before the Court of Appeals decides (MCR 7.303, 7.305; MCR 9.108)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Michigan Court of Appeals","d":"Statewide intermediate court deciding in three-judge panels. Hears appeals of right from final judgments of the circuit court and the Court of Claims, appeals by leave from interlocutory orders and from circuit-court appellate decisions, and some original actions (MCR 7.203). Published opinions bind later panels; unpublished ones do not (MCR 7.215(C), (J))."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Courts (incl. family division and business court dockets)","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts organized by judicial circuit. Some circuits keep business court dockets for business or commercial disputes defined in MCL 600.8031, and pleadings must state whether the case qualifies (MCR 2.112(O)). Circuit courts also hear appeals from district and probate courts and agencies (MCR 7.101–7.123)."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Court of Claims","d":"Hears claims against the state and its agencies. A defendant may move such a case from circuit court by notice of transfer under MCR 2.228. Its final judgments are appealable of right to the Court of Appeals (MCR 7.203(A)(1))."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"District Courts, Probate Courts, and Municipal Courts","d":"District courts handle civil infraction actions, landlord-tenant and land-contract summary proceedings, small claims, and other civil matters within their statutory limits (Chapter 4 of the MCR); discovery there requires leave or stipulation (MCR 2.301(A)). Probate courts handle estates, trusts, guardianships, and mental-health matters (Chapter 5). Appeals from both go first to the circuit court (MCR 7.103–7.104). Dollar limits of district court jurisdiction are set by statute and were not collected."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute and scope: Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (eff. March 24, 2026)","d":"Michigan now has an anti-SLAPP law. The Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, 2025 PA 52, took effect March 24, 2026, and applies to civil actions filed, or causes of action asserted, on or after that date (Sec. 13).\nIt covers claims based on (i) a communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (ii) a communication on an issue under consideration or review in such a proceeding; or (iii) the exercise of speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights under the U.S. or Michigan Constitution on a matter of public concern.\nExcluded: claims against sellers or lessors of goods or services arising from communications about their sale or lease (creative, journalistic, and political works are carved back in), and claims by individuals under listed civil-rights, employment, whistleblower, FOIA, and workers’ compensation statutes (Elliott-Larsen, PWDCRA, WPA, WDCA, Michigan FOIA, Title VII, Title IX, ADEA, ADA, FMLA, FLSA). The Act must be broadly construed (Sec. 11).\nNote: an earlier assumption that Michigan has no anti-SLAPP statute is out of date.","c":"MCL 691.1852, 691.1861, 691.1863 (2025 PA 52, secs. 2, 11, 13); MCR 2.114(A)","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: special motion within 60 days; automatic stay","d":"File a ‘special motion for expedited relief’ to dismiss all or part of the action no later than 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim, or later for good cause. Under MCR 2.114 it must meet MCR 2.119 requirements, say it is a special motion under MCL 691.1853 et seq., and state the basis under MCL 691.1857.\nFiling stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery and pending motions. On motion, the court may stay related hearings or discovery involving other parties. The stay lasts until the ruling and the time to appeal expire. The court may allow limited, specific discovery. A fee motion is not stayed, and parties can still voluntarily dismiss or sever.\nThe court must hear the motion within 60 days after filing (or within 60 days after ordering limited discovery, or later for good cause) and must rule within 60 days after the hearing.","c":"MCL 691.1853–691.1855, 691.1858; MCR 2.114(B)–(D)","v":true},{"t":"Standard: dismissal with prejudice unless the plaintiff makes a prima facie case","d":"The court considers the pleadings, motion papers, affidavits, depositions, admissions, and other documentary evidence. It must dismiss with prejudice if (a) the movant shows the claim is eligible, (b) the responding party fails to show an exclusion applies, and (c) either the responding party fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact.","c":"MCL 691.1856, 691.1857(1); MCR 2.114(D)(2)–(3)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory for a winning movant","d":"The court must award court costs, reasonable attorney fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant. A prevailing respondent gets them only if the motion was frivolous or filed solely to delay. A voluntary dismissal with prejudice counts as the movant prevailing; a dismissal without prejudice does not stop the movant from getting a ruling and fees.","c":"MCL 691.1857(2)–(3), 691.1860; MCR 2.114(F)","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: as of right within 21 days; whole case stayed","d":"The statute gives the moving party an appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part, filed within 21 days after entry. MCR 2.114(E) goes further, allowing the movant to appeal as of right from an order ‘denying or granting, in whole or in part’, filed under MCR 7.204. Once any party appeals the ruling, all proceedings between all parties are stayed until the appeal ends (stays on appeal are governed by MCR 7.209 and 7.305(J)).\nThe rule does not give the responding party (plaintiff) an appeal of right from a grant that leaves claims pending; a full dismissal is a final judgment.","c":"MCL 691.1859, 691.1854(3); MCR 2.114(C)(4), (E); MCR 7.204(A)(1)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Temporary admission (pro hac vice) under MCR 8.126: State Bar first, then the tribunal","d":"A ‘foreign attorney’ (licensed in another U.S. state or territory, D.C., a Tribal court, or a foreign country, not disbarred or suspended anywhere) appears only through a sponsoring Michigan lawyer, who must appear as counsel of record and file a motion with the tribunal. Admission is discretionary.\nBefore filing with the tribunal, file the motion and supporting papers with the State Bar of Michigan with the fee. The Bar reports to the tribunal within 7 days; the tribunal may not enter an order until it receives the Bar’s report. Supporting papers include a certificate of good standing issued within the last 30 days, an affidavit (licenses, good standing, no discipline pending, familiarity with the MRPC, Michigan Rules of Evidence, and court rules), any disciplinary dispositions, and the sponsor’s statement. File the granting order with the Bar within 7 days.","c":"MCR 8.126(A)–(C)","v":true},{"t":"Fee and the 5-case limit","d":"The fee equals the discipline and client-protection portions of a State Bar member’s annual dues. The rule does not state a dollar figure; check the State Bar’s current amount.\nA foreign attorney may not appear in more than five cases in any 365-day period. The limit (and, for certain public defender, legal-aid, and clinic lawyers, repeat fees) does not apply while a BLE Rule 5 application for admission without examination is pending.\nBy applying, the lawyer consents to Michigan’s disciplinary jurisdiction, and must notify the Bar and tribunals within 7 days after losing good standing anywhere.","c":"MCR 8.126(B)(2)–(4), (C)(2), (C)(6)–(7)","v":true},{"t":"Scope: whole case through appeals; separate admission for agencies and arbitration","d":"Temporary admission granted by a court lasts for the entire case, including appeals, remands, and court-ordered facilitation, mediation, or arbitration. An arbitrator or agency may admit a foreign attorney only for that proceeding; if it turns into a court case, apply again.\nThe tribunal may revoke admission for misconduct and must revoke on loss of good standing. If the sponsor withdraws, another Michigan lawyer must step in unless the tribunal waives it; the sponsor must be able to conduct the case.\nA narrow waiver applies to lawyers for Indian tribes in child custody proceedings under the Michigan Indian Family Preservation Act.","c":"MCR 8.126(D)–(F), (H)","v":true},{"t":"Admission without examination (BLE Rule 5)","d":"A lawyer licensed in a U.S. jurisdiction who is in good standing and intends to practice (or teach full-time) in Michigan may be admitted without exam after practicing law as a principal occupation, teaching full-time at a law school, or serving as an active-duty judge advocate for 3 of the 5 years before applying. Applicants must also complete any Michigan Law Component required by BLE Rule 3a and submit an NCBE character report.\nOnce a certificate issues, the applicant must take the oath and join the State Bar within 3 years. In-house and institutional lawyers who do not meet the practice requirement may seek a special certificate tied to their current employment.","c":"BLE Rule 5(B)–(F)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary and multijurisdictional practice without admission","d":"MRPC 5.5(c)–(d) follows the ABA safe harbors for temporary practice by U.S.-admitted lawyers, such as work with active local co-counsel or work related to a proceeding or ADR. MRPC 5.5(e) also lets a lawyer licensed elsewhere practice the law of that home jurisdiction while physically in Michigan, provided the lawyer does not hold out a Michigan office or offer or provide Michigan legal services unless otherwise permitted.","c":"MRPC 5.5(c)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances and ghostwriting","d":"A Michigan-licensed lawyer may limit the scope of representation and file a notice of limited appearance in a civil action that identifies the limits by date, time period, or subject matter. A limited-scope lawyer may later withdraw by notice: it takes effect immediately if the client signs it, or 14 days after filing and service unless the client files a written objection that the agreed services were not completed.\nA lawyer who only drafts documents does not appear and must not sign them, but each document must carry the statement: ‘This document was drafted or partially drafted with the assistance of a lawyer licensed to practice in the State of Michigan, pursuant to Michigan Rule of Professional Conduct 1.2(b).’","c":"MRPC 1.2(b); MCR 2.117(B)(2)(c)–(d), (C)(4), (D)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing through MiFILE","d":"Attorneys must e-file the required case types in courts where e-filing has been implemented, unless exempt because of a disability. Self-represented filers must e-file only in courts that SCAO has approved to mandate it. An e-filed document is deemed filed when transmission is complete and fees are paid or waived. Anything submitted by 11:59 p.m. on a business day is filed that day; weekend, holiday, and court-closed-day submissions are filed the next business day.\nDates of birth, Social Security numbers, driver’s license and state ID numbers, passport numbers, and financial account numbers are protected personal identifying information and must not appear in public filings. Where an SSN is required, use only the last four digits.","c":"MCR 1.109(G)(3)(f)–(h), (G)(5)(b); MCR 1.109(D)(9)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Michigan Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"Adopted by the Michigan Supreme Court and drawn largely from the ABA Model Rules, with Michigan changes. The collected version is updated with orders effective January 1, 2026. Only the rule text is authoritative: the comments do not expand or limit the rules (MRPC 1.0(c)). A violation is a basis for discipline but creates no civil cause of action. Discipline is prosecuted by the Attorney Grievance Commission (MCR 9.108).","c":"MRPC 1.0(a)–(c); MCR 9.108(A)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality uses the older ‘confidences and secrets’ model","d":"Unlike ABA Model Rule 1.6 (‘information relating to the representation’), MRPC 1.6 protects ‘confidences’ (privileged information) and ‘secrets’ (other information the client asked to keep or whose disclosure would embarrass or harm the client).\nPermissive disclosure covers client consent after full disclosure; disclosure required by law or court order; rectifying the consequences of a client’s illegal or fraudulent act in which the lawyer’s services were used; the client’s intention to commit a crime, plus information needed to prevent it (any crime, not limited to death, bodily harm, or financial injury); and fee collection or self-defense.","c":"MRPC 1.6(a)–(c)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: contingent-fee cap and looser fee-splitting","d":"In personal injury, wrongful death, and no-fault benefits matters, a contingent fee above one-third of the net recovery is a ‘clearly excessive fee’ under MRPC 1.5(a) (MCR 8.121(A)–(C)).\nA fee may be divided between lawyers in different firms if the client is advised of and does not object to every lawyer’s participation and the total fee is reasonable. The rule does not require the ABA’s proportional-or-joint-responsibility condition or written client consent.\nThe MRPC 1.5(a) test is ‘clearly excessive’: a lawyer of ordinary prudence would have a definite and firm conviction that the fee exceeds a reasonable fee.","c":"MRPC 1.5(a), (c), (e); MCR 8.121","v":true},{"t":"Scope of representation: Michigan-specific additions","d":"MRPC 1.2(a) adds that a lawyer does not violate the rule by granting opposing counsel’s reasonable requests that do not prejudice the client, being punctual, or avoiding offensive tactics. The client also decides whether to accept a mediation evaluation. MRPC 1.2(b) adds the limited-appearance and ghostwriting provisions described under out-of-state practice.","c":"MRPC 1.2(a)–(b)","v":true},{"t":"Candor: remedial-measures duty tracks the Model Rule, with a Michigan twist","d":"MRPC 3.3(a)–(c) matches the ABA structure: a duty to take reasonable remedial measures, including disclosure to the tribunal if needed, that lasts to the end of the proceeding even if MRPC 1.6 information must be revealed. MRPC 3.3(e) adds black-letter text on false evidence: first try to persuade the client, then take remedial measures, and seek to withdraw if that will fix it.","c":"MRPC 3.3(a)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"Trial publicity lists presumptively prejudicial topics in the rule itself","d":"MRPC 3.6(a) says an extrajudicial statement is likely to be materially prejudicial when it concerns a civil matter triable to a jury, a criminal matter, or a proceeding that could result in incarceration, and relates to listed topics such as a party’s or witness’s character, credibility, or criminal record, test results, or likely-inadmissible information. In the ABA Model Rules, comparable topics are listed only in a comment.","c":"MRPC 3.6(a)","v":true}],"style":"State of Michigan","home":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"21 days to appeal, and it is jurisdictional","d":"A civil appeal of right must be filed within 21 days after entry of the judgment or order, not 30. Only a postjudgment motion for new trial, rehearing, reconsideration, or other relief filed within that initial 21-day period (or extra time the trial court allows within it) restarts the clock. After that, only a delayed application for leave (within 6 months) is available, and it is discretionary.","c":"MCR 7.204(A)(1); MCR 7.205(A)(4)"},{"t":"Summons expires in 91 days and the case dismisses itself","d":"A summons expires 91 days after issuance. Unserved defendants are dismissed without prejudice automatically, with no court order needed. To extend, get a second summons by court order within the 91 days, showing due diligence; the extension may not run beyond 1 year from the original issuance.","c":"MCR 2.102(D)–(E)"},{"t":"Personal-jurisdiction and service defenses die with your first filing","d":"Lack of personal jurisdiction and insufficient process or service must be raised in your first motion under MCR 2.116 or your responsive pleading, whichever is filed first, or they are waived. Release, statute of limitations, statute of frauds, and arbitration-agreement defenses ((C)(7)) must be in the responsive pleading or an earlier motion.","c":"MCR 2.116(D)(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Rejecting an offer of judgment can cost you the other side’s attorney fees","d":"Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 68, any party can make an offer, and the sanction includes a reasonable attorney fee from rejection forward. Failing to accept within 21 days is a rejection. Do the adjusted-verdict math before letting an offer lapse.","c":"MCR 2.405(B)–(D)"},{"t":"Motion papers: 20 pages combined, no reply brief without permission","d":"A motion and its brief together, or a response and its brief, may not exceed 20 double-spaced pages, and reply or supplemental briefs are barred unless the court permits. Default timing: serve a motion at least 9 days before the hearing by mail (7 by delivery) and file at least 7 days before. Serve a response at least 5 days before by mail (3 by delivery) and file at least 3 days before. Check local and judge practice, and summary disposition has its own longer schedule.","c":"MCR 2.119(A)(2), (C); MCR 2.116(G)(1)"},{"t":"Reconsideration in 21 days, and only for ‘palpable error’","d":"A motion for rehearing or reconsideration is due within 21 days after the order. It gets no response or argument unless ordered, and it must show a palpable error that misled the court and parties and would change the result. Rearguing the same issues fails.","c":"MCR 2.119(F)"},{"t":"The ‘7-day rule’ for proposed orders","d":"If an order is not entered at the hearing, a party may serve a proposed order with notice that it will be submitted for signature unless written objections are filed within 7 days. Ignore it and the order enters as drafted. The party who obtains a signed judgment or order must serve it on all parties within 7 days.","c":"MCR 2.602(B)(3), (E)(1)"},{"t":"Interrogatories capped at 20, and initial disclosures come early","d":"Each separately represented party may serve no more than 20 interrogatories on each party, counting discrete subparts. Initial disclosures, including legal theories and expert subject areas, are due without a request: a pleader serves within 14 days after an opposing party answers, and an answering party within the later of 14 days after those are due or 28 days after answering; discovery generally cannot be sought until you serve your own.","c":"MCR 2.309(A)(2); MCR 2.302(A)(1), (A)(5); MCR 2.301(A)"},{"t":"Setting aside a default needs a verified meritorious defense","d":"Except for lack of jurisdiction, a motion to set aside a default or default judgment requires good cause plus a verified statement of facts showing a meritorious defense. If you were personally served, file before the default judgment or within 21 days after it (MCR 2.612 relief aside). Setting aside is conditioned on paying taxable costs.","c":"MCR 2.603(D)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice: Bar filing first, 5 cases per 365 days","d":"Filing a pro hac vice motion straight with the court will not work. The papers and fee go to the State Bar first, the Bar reports within 7 days, and the court cannot grant the motion until it gets that report. Out-of-state lawyers are limited to five temporary admissions in any 365-day period unless a BLE Rule 5 application is pending.","c":"MCR 8.126(B)(2)–(3), (C)(2)–(3)"},{"t":"Unpublished Court of Appeals opinions: not binding, and citing them has rules","d":"Unpublished opinions are not binding precedent and should not be cited where published authority exists. When citing one, explain why and include the docket number and date (attach a copy if issued before 1996). Published opinions issued on or after November 1, 1990 bind later panels until reversed or overruled through the conflict-panel procedure.","c":"MCR 7.215(C), (J)(1); MCR 2.119(A)(2)"},{"t":"Protected personal identifying information stays out of public filings","d":"Do not put dates of birth, Social Security numbers, driver’s license or state ID numbers, passport numbers, or financial account numbers in public court filings. Use the redaction and SCAO-form procedure in MCR 1.109(D)(9); where an SSN is required, use only the last four digits.","c":"MCR 1.109(D)(9)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory challenge of a judge in civil cases","d":"The Michigan Court Rules have no peremptory or as-of-right substitution of judge. A judge is removed only by a disqualification motion for cause under MCR 2.003. (The rules’ only ‘peremptory challenges’ are for jurors, MCR 2.511(F).)","c":"MCR 2.003; MCR 2.511(F)","v":true},{"t":"Grounds for disqualification","d":"Grounds include actual bias or prejudice for or against a party or attorney; an objective ‘serious risk of actual bias’ under Caperton v Massey, or failure to meet the appearance-of-impropriety standard in Canon 2 of the Michigan Code of Judicial Conduct; personal knowledge of disputed facts; prior work as a lawyer in the matter; having been a partner of or lawyer for a party, or in a firm representing a party, within the preceding 2 years; more than a de minimis economic interest; and family relationships within the third degree.","c":"MCR 2.003(C)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Strict timing: 14 days from discovering the grounds","d":"In the trial courts, move within 14 days of discovering the grounds, or forthwith if discovered within 14 days of trial. In the Court of Appeals, move within 14 days of disclosure of the panel assignment or discovery of the grounds. In the Supreme Court, file with the application or within 28 days. Include all known grounds and an affidavit. Late motions may be granted for good cause, but untimeliness counts against the motion.","c":"MCR 2.003(D)(1)–(2)","v":true},{"t":"Who decides: the challenged judge, then de novo review on request","d":"The challenged judge rules first. If the motion is denied, a party may ask that it be referred to the chief judge (or, in a single-judge court or when the challenged judge is chief judge, to the State Court Administrator for assignment), who decides de novo. In the Supreme Court the challenged justice decides and publishes reasons, and a party may ask the full Court to decide de novo within 14 days.","c":"MCR 2.003(D)(1)(c), (D)(3)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"UPEPA: broad construction and creative-work carve-back","d":"The anti-SLAPP act directs that it be broadly construed to protect speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights under both constitutions. Its commercial-speech exclusion does not reach creating, disseminating, exhibiting, or advertising dramatic, literary, musical, political, journalistic, or artistic works.","c":"MCL 691.1852(1)(a), (2)(a); MCL 691.1861","v":true},{"t":"Michigan Constitution free speech clause: not collected","d":"Whether Michigan courts read Const 1963, art 1, § 5 more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked against collected sources.","c":"Const 1963, art 1, § 5 (not in collected texts)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Summary disposition replaces both Rule 12(b) and Rule 56","d":"Michigan uses a single motion for summary disposition on ten grounds, MCR 2.116(C)(1)–(10). Jurisdiction, process, and service defenses ((C)(1)–(3)) are waived unless raised in the first motion or the responsive pleading, whichever comes first.","c":"MCR 2.116(C), (D)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Offers to stipulate to judgment shift attorney fees","d":"Any party may serve an offer up to 28 days before trial. A party that rejects an offer and does worse than the average of offer and counteroffer pays the offeror’s actual costs, including a reasonable attorney fee. The court may decline to award the fee in the interest of justice.","c":"MCR 2.405(A)–(D)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Michigan Court Rules of 1985, Chapter 7 (Appellate Rules), MCR 7.101–7.319, and related MCR 1.108, 1.109, 2.114, 2.119, 2.602, 2.604, 2.611, 2.614, from the official Michigan Supreme Court compilation “Updated September 2, 2026” (Chapter 7 “Chapter Updated September 1, 2026”); Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, 2025 PA 52, MCL 691.1859. Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Michigan: 21 days, a claim of appeal, and a lot of leave applications","d":"Civil appeals from the circuit court and the Court of Claims go to the Court of Appeals, and from there to the Michigan Supreme Court by discretionary application for leave to appeal. District and probate court decisions generally go first to the circuit court (MCR 7.101–7.123).\nThe defining number is 21. A claim of appeal from a final judgment must be filed within 21 days after entry, and the deadline is jurisdictional. Only a postjudgment motion filed within those same 21 days restarts the clock. Anything that is not a ‘final judgment or final order’ as defined in MCR 7.202(6) needs an application for leave, also due in 21 days, with a 6-month delayed-application safety valve. Supreme Court applications are due 42 days after the Court of Appeals decision in civil cases."},"groups":[["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Appeal of right from a final judgment: 21 days, jurisdictional","d":"An aggrieved party may appeal as of right from a final judgment or final order of the circuit court or Court of Claims, but not from a circuit-court judgment on appeal from another court. File a claim of appeal and the entry fee with the Court of Appeals within 21 days after entry. ‘Entry’ means the date the judgment is signed, or data entry in the register of actions. The time limit is jurisdictional, and MCR 1.108 computation applies.","c":"MCR 7.203(A)(1); MCR 7.204(A)(1), (B)"},{"t":"What counts as ‘final’","d":"A final judgment or order is the first one that disposes of all claims and adjudicates the rights and liabilities of all parties. Also final: an order designated final under MCR 2.604(B) (receivership and similar actions), certain postjudgment custody orders, postjudgment orders awarding or denying attorney fees and costs, orders denying governmental immunity (under MCR 2.116(C)(7) or a (C)(10) motion based on immunity), and certain tax-foreclosure surplus-proceeds orders. Appeals from the listed postjudgment orders are limited to the part appealable of right.","c":"MCR 7.202(6)(a); MCR 7.203(A)(1)"},{"t":"What to file with the claim","d":"With the claim of appeal, file the judgment or order appealed from; the court reporter’s certificate (or a statement that the transcript was ordered or that there is none); proof of service; any bond; the lower court register of actions; and the jurisdictional checklist. Within the same 21 days, file a copy of the claim, any fee and bond, and the reporter’s certificate in the trial court, and serve all parties. Cases with custody, guardianship, or a ruling invalidating a state constitutional provision, statute, or rule must say so in capitals on the claim.","c":"MCR 7.204(C)–(F)"},{"t":"Late service of the judgment","d":"If the judgment was served later than MCR 2.602 requires (7 days after signing), file an affidavit with the claim. The claim is deemed timely if the court finds service was late and the claim was filed within 14 days after service. The appellee may oppose by affidavit within 14 days.","c":"MCR 7.204(A)(3); MCR 2.602(E)(1)"},{"t":"Appellee appearance and docketing statement","d":"An appellee may file an appearance within 14 days after service of the claim; without one, the appellee is not entitled to notice. In all civil appeals the appellant must file a docketing statement within 28 days after the claim is filed, and failure may lead to dismissal.","c":"MCR 7.204(G)–(H)"},{"t":"Appeal by leave: interlocutory orders and everything else","d":"Leave is required for non-final orders of the circuit court and Court of Claims, for circuit-court judgments on appeal from other courts, for agency orders reviewable by the Court of Appeals, and when a timely appeal of right was missed. Applications are due 21 days after entry (or after an order deciding a timely postjudgment motion). The application must show, for an interlocutory order, how waiting for final judgment would cause substantial harm.","c":"MCR 7.203(B); MCR 7.205(A)(1), (B)(1)"},{"t":"Delayed application for leave: 6 months, discretionary","d":"If a civil appeal of right or application was not filed in time, a delayed application for leave may be filed within 6 months after entry of the judgment or order. It must explain the reasons for delay, and the court may weigh the length of and reasons for the delay. If the Court of Appeals dismisses a timely claim of appeal for lack of jurisdiction, a delayed application may also be filed within 21 days after the dismissal order (or the order denying reconsideration).","c":"MCR 7.205(A)(4)"}]],["Tolling and postjudgment motions",[{"t":"Only a motion filed within the first 21 days tolls","d":"The appeal period runs from the order deciding a postjudgment motion for new trial, rehearing, reconsideration, or other relief only if the motion was filed within the initial 21-day appeal period, or within further time the trial court allowed for good cause during that period.","c":"MCR 7.204(A)(1)(d); MCR 7.205(A)(1)(b)"},{"t":"Reconsideration of the postjudgment ruling does not toll again","d":"A motion for rehearing or reconsideration of an order deciding a tolling postjudgment motion does not extend the appeal time unless it was itself filed within the 21-day period.","c":"MCR 7.204(A)(2) (closing paragraph)"},{"t":"Trial-court motion deadlines that match","d":"Motions for new trial or to alter or amend a judgment are due within 21 days after entry of judgment. Motions for reconsideration of a motion decision are also due within 21 days and must show palpable error.","c":"MCR 2.611(B); MCR 2.119(F)"},{"t":"Trial court loses power to amend once the appeal is filed","d":"After a claim of appeal is filed or leave is granted, the trial court may not set aside or amend the judgment except by Court of Appeals order, by stipulation, after a merits decision in a case with a preliminary injunction, or as otherwise provided by law.","c":"MCR 7.208(A)"}]],["Cross-appeals",[{"t":"Cross-appeal within 21 days","d":"Any appellee may cross-appeal when an appeal of right is filed or leave is granted. In multi-party cases, any party may cross-appeal against any other party. File the claim of cross-appeal and the entry fee within 21 days after the claim of appeal is filed or served on the cross-appellant, whichever is later. If the case began by application, file within 21 days after the clerk certifies the order granting leave.","c":"MCR 7.207(A)–(B)"},{"t":"Cross-appeal survives dismissal of the main appeal","d":"If the appellant abandons the appeal or it is dismissed, the cross-appeal continues. The cross-appellant must file a court reporter’s certificate within 21 days after the dismissal order if there is a record to transcribe. A cross-appellant also files its own docketing statement within 28 days, and a late cross-appeal requires an application under MCR 7.205.","c":"MCR 7.207(C)–(E); MCR 7.204(H)(3)"}]],["Record and transcripts",[{"t":"Transcripts: the appellant’s job","d":"The appellant must order the transcript and secure its filing. Court reporters have 91 days after ordering to file it in ordinary civil cases (shorter in custody and certain criminal appeals). Parties holding exhibits must file them with the trial court within 21 days after the claim of appeal. Within 21 days after the transcript is filed, the appellant serves the entire record on each appellee.","c":"MCR 7.210(B)(1), (B)(3), (C), (F)"},{"t":"No transcript available: settled record","d":"If transcripts are unavailable, the appellant moves in the trial court to settle the record with a proposed statement of facts within 14 days after filing the claim of appeal. Where only some transcripts are available, the deadline is 56 days after filing them (28 in custody and interlocutory criminal appeals).","c":"MCR 7.210(B)(2)"}]],["Briefs",[{"t":"Appellant’s brief: 56 days, extendable once by stipulation","d":"In ordinary civil appeals the appellant’s brief is due 56 days after the latest of: the claim of appeal, certification of the order granting leave, filing of the transcript, or filing of a settled statement of facts. The parties may extend it 28 days by signed stipulation filed with the court, and the court may extend on motion. Custody and guardianship cases use 28 days, extendable only by motion.","c":"MCR 7.212(A)(1)(a)"},{"t":"Appellee’s brief: 35 days; reply: 21 days","d":"The appellee’s brief is due 35 days after service of the appellant’s brief (21 days in custody and similar cases), extendable 28 days by stipulation. A reply brief is due within 21 days after service of the appellee’s brief and is limited to rebuttal. A late brief forfeits oral argument, and filing a motion does not stay brief deadlines.","c":"MCR 7.212(A)(2), (A)(4)–(5), (G)"},{"t":"Length and form: 16,000 words; reply 3,200","d":"Principal briefs are limited to 16,000 words and reply briefs to 3,200 words. The count includes the statement of facts, arguments, and relief, plus footnotes and text in embedded graphics. State the word count after the signature block. Use 1-inch margins, 12-point font, and 1.5-line spacing. Motions to exceed the limit are due with the brief and are disfavored. Self-represented filers without word processing get 50 pages (10 for a reply).","c":"MCR 7.212(B), (G)"},{"t":"Required contents","d":"Required contents include a title page stating whether oral argument is requested, a table of contents, an index of authorities, a statement of the basis of jurisdiction with key dates (signing or entry, tolling motions), questions presented with the trial court’s answers, a statement of facts with record page cites, and arguments that each state the standard of review and show preservation. The appellee must say whether the jurisdictional summary and standard of review are correct.","c":"MCR 7.212(C)–(D)"},{"t":"Amicus","d":"Amicus briefs on the merits need a motion granted by the court, filed within 21 days after the appellee’s brief, with no fee. Government entities listed in the rule are exempt from the motion requirement.","c":"MCR 7.212(H)"}]],["Stays and bonds",[{"t":"No execution for 21 days; appeal alone does not stay","d":"Enforcement is automatically stayed for 21 days after a final judgment, and through decision of a timely postjudgment motion plus 21 days. After that, an appeal does not stay enforcement unless a court orders it. Injunctions and receivership orders are enforceable immediately unless the court orders otherwise.","c":"MCR 2.614(A); MCR 7.209(A)(1), (E)(1)"},{"t":"Money judgments: 110% bond buys an automatic stay","d":"Except in domestic relations matters, filing a bond under MCR 3.604 of at least 110% of the judgment, including costs, interest, attorney fees, and sanctions to date, stays a money judgment automatically. The trial court may also grant a stay with no bond or a reduced bond, or accept other security such as an irrevocable letter of credit. A motion for bond or stay must be decided by the trial court before it can be brought in the Court of Appeals.","c":"MCR 7.209(A)(2)–(3), (E)(2)–(4)"},{"t":"Governmental immunity and anti-SLAPP appeals stay the case","d":"An appeal from denial of governmental immunity automatically stays all proceedings. Under the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, an appeal from an order ruling on a special motion stays all proceedings between all parties until the appeal concludes.","c":"MCR 7.209(A)(1); MCR 2.614(D); MCL 691.1854(3); MCR 2.114(C)(4)"}]],["Motions, decisions, and reconsideration",[{"t":"Answers to motions","d":"Answers to motions to dismiss, remand, or affirm are due 21 days after service. Answers to motions for peremptory reversal are due in 35 days. Answers to motions for reconsideration, stay, to strike, for amicus filing, or for abeyance are due in 14 days. A motion for immediate consideration can shorten the schedule.","c":"MCR 7.211(B)(2), (C)(6)"},{"t":"Applications are decided on the papers","d":"There is no oral argument on an application for leave. The court may grant, deny, enter final relief, or request more record. If leave is granted, the case proceeds as an appeal of right: cross-appeal, docketing statement (28 days), and reporter certificate (14 days) deadlines run from certification of the grant. Unless ordered otherwise, the appeal is limited to the issues in the application. Answers to applications are due 21 days after service.","c":"MCR 7.205(C), (E)"},{"t":"Reconsideration: 21 days, 3,200 words, no second try","d":"A motion for reconsideration of a Court of Appeals opinion or order may be filed within 21 days after the order or the date stamped on the opinion, in a single document of no more than 3,200 words. Answers are due within 14 days and are limited to 2,500 words. Late motions and motions to reconsider a denial of reconsideration are not accepted. A chief-judge dismissal for lack of jurisdiction has its own 21-day reconsideration motion to a three-judge panel.","c":"MCR 7.215(I); MCR 7.203(F)(2)"},{"t":"Publication and precedent","d":"Unpublished opinions are not binding. Any party may ask, by letter within 21 days after release, that an opinion be published, and other parties may respond within 14 days. A panel must follow published Court of Appeals decisions issued on or after November 1, 1990; a panel that disagrees must say so, which can trigger a conflict-panel poll.","c":"MCR 7.215(C)–(D), (J)"},{"t":"When the judgment takes effect","d":"A Court of Appeals judgment takes effect after the time to apply to the Supreme Court expires, or after Supreme Court disposition if an application is filed, unless the court orders immediate effect. Enforcement proceeds in the trial court after the record is returned.","c":"MCR 7.215(E)–(F)"}]],["Review in the Michigan Supreme Court",[{"t":"Discretionary application for leave","d":"The Supreme Court reviews Court of Appeals cases by application for leave to appeal. Grounds include a substantial question about the validity of a legislative act, significant public interest in a case involving the state, a legal principle of major significance to state jurisprudence, or a Court of Appeals decision that is clearly erroneous and will cause material injustice or conflicts with Supreme Court or other Court of Appeals decisions. The application must follow MCR 7.212(B) length rules.","c":"MCR 7.303(B)(1); MCR 7.305(A)–(B)"},{"t":"Timing: 42 days civil; 28 days interlocutory; bypass","d":"Applications are due 42 days in civil cases (56 in criminal) after the Court of Appeals decision, its order denying a timely reconsideration motion, or its grant of publication. Applications from non-final Court of Appeals orders, including orders granting leave, are due within 28 days. A bypass application before Court of Appeals decision is due within 42 days after the Court of Appeals filing. The answer is due 28 days after service; the reply, limited to 3,200 words, within 21 days.","c":"MCR 7.305(C)–(E)"},{"t":"Remand decisions","d":"If the Court of Appeals remands, a party may apply within 42 days (civil) after the remand decision or after the decision following remand, and then may raise all original issues. An application stays remand proceedings only when the remand decision is a judgment under MCR 7.215(E)(1), unless ordered otherwise.","c":"MCR 7.305(C)(5)–(6)"},{"t":"Briefing after leave is granted","d":"Briefing follows the order granting leave. Calendar cases: appellant 56 days, appellee 35 days after service, reply 21 days. Cases argued on the application: 42, 21, and 14 days. Form and length follow MCR 7.212, and an argument over 6,500 words on one issue needs a summary of argument.","c":"MCR 7.312(A)–(B), (E)"},{"t":"Rehearing","d":"A motion for rehearing of a Supreme Court opinion is due within 21 days after the opinion is filed and is limited to 16,000 words. It is subject to the palpable-error standard of MCR 2.119(F)(3).","c":"MCR 7.311(F)(1)"},{"t":"Certified questions and other jurisdiction","d":"The Supreme Court may also answer certified questions, issue advisory opinions on request, and exercise superintending control.","c":"MCR 7.303(B)(3)–(5); MCR 7.308"}]],["Interlocutory and special appeals",[{"t":"Interlocutory orders: leave required, show substantial harm","d":"Non-final circuit court orders are reviewable only by leave, applied for within 21 days. The application must show how waiting for final judgment would cause substantial harm. A motion for immediate consideration is available when a decision is needed by a date certain.","c":"MCR 7.203(B)(1); MCR 7.205(A)(1), (B)(1), (F)"},{"t":"Partial judgments","d":"An order adjudicating fewer than all claims or parties is not final, and the jurisdictional statement must show why the court has jurisdiction. MCR 2.604(B) designations of finality are limited to receivership and similar actions.","c":"MCR 7.202(6)(a)(i)–(ii); MCR 7.212(C)(4)(b); MCR 2.604"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP special motions: appeal of right","d":"The Uniform Public Expression Protection Act gives the moving party an appeal of right from an order denying a special motion in whole or in part, due within 21 days. MCR 2.114(E) extends the moving party’s appeal of right to orders ‘denying or granting, in whole or in part,’ filed under MCR 7.204.","c":"MCL 691.1859; MCR 2.114(E)"},{"t":"Governmental immunity denials are appealable of right","d":"An order denying governmental immunity to a governmental party under MCR 2.116(C)(7), or a (C)(10) motion based on immunity, is a ‘final order,’ and the appeal automatically stays the case.","c":"MCR 7.202(6)(a)(v); MCR 7.209(A)(1)"},{"t":"Business court assignment orders are not appealable","d":"An order about assigning a case to the business court may not be appealed to the Court of Appeals.","c":"MCR 7.203(D)"}]],["E-filing and computation",[{"t":"Filing date and e-filing","d":"In the Court of Appeals, the ‘date of filing’ is the date the clerk receives the document or, if e-filed, the date it is deemed filed under MCR 1.109(G)(5)(b). Submissions by 11:59 p.m. on a business day count that day; weekend, holiday, and court-closed submissions count the next business day. Attorneys must e-file the required case types where e-filing is implemented.","c":"MCR 7.202(2), (4); MCR 1.109(G)(3)(f), (G)(5)(b)"},{"t":"Computing time","d":"Exclude the trigger day and include the last day, unless it falls on a Saturday, Sunday, legal holiday, or court-closed day, in which case the deadline moves to the next such day that is open.","c":"MCR 1.108(1)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Claim of appeal (civil appeal of right)","d":"21 days from entry of the final judgment or order, or entry of an order deciding a timely postjudgment motion.\nJurisdictional. File in the Court of Appeals with the entry fee.","c":"MCR 7.204(A)(1)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the final judgment or order, or entry of an order deciding a timely postjudgment motion)","note":"Jurisdictional. File in the Court of Appeals with the entry fee."}},{"t":"Application for leave to appeal (Court of Appeals)","d":"21 days from entry of the order or judgment, or of an order deciding a timely postjudgment motion.\nJurisdictional.","c":"MCR 7.205(A)(1)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order or judgment, or of an order deciding a timely postjudgment motion)","note":"Jurisdictional."}},{"t":"Delayed application for leave (civil)","d":"6 months\nDiscretionary; explain the delay.","c":"MCR 7.205(A)(4)(a)"},{"t":"Delayed application after dismissal of a timely claim for lack of jurisdiction","d":"21 days from entry of the dismissal order or order denying reconsideration.","c":"MCR 7.205(A)(4)(b)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the dismissal order or order denying reconsideration)","note":""}},{"t":"Tolling postjudgment motion (new trial, reconsideration, relief)","d":"21 days from entry of judgment.\nMust be filed within the initial 21-day period to toll.","c":"MCR 7.204(A)(1)(d); MCR 2.611(B); MCR 2.119(F)(1)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Must be filed within the initial 21-day period to toll."}},{"t":"Late-served judgment: claim of appeal","d":"14 days from service of the judgment (if served late under MCR 2.602).\nRequires an affidavit showing late service.","c":"MCR 7.204(A)(3)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the judgment)","note":"Requires an affidavit showing late service."}},{"t":"Appellee appearance","d":"14 days from service of the claim of appeal.\nOptional, but needed to receive notice.","c":"MCR 7.204(G)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the claim of appeal)","note":"Optional, but needed to receive notice."}},{"t":"Docketing statement","d":"28 days from filing of the claim of appeal (or cross-appeal, or certification of order granting leave).\nCivil appeals only.","c":"MCR 7.204(H); MCR 7.205(E)(3)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the claim of appeal)","note":"Civil appeals only."}},{"t":"Claim of cross-appeal","d":"21 days from later of filing or service of the claim of appeal; or certification of order granting leave.","c":"MCR 7.207(B)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (later of filing or service of the claim of appeal; or certification of order granting leave)","note":""}},{"t":"Answer to application for leave (Court of Appeals)","d":"21 days from service of the application.","c":"MCR 7.205(C)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (service of the application)","note":""}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief (ordinary civil)","d":"56 days from latest of claim of appeal, certified leave order, transcript filing, or settled statement.\nOne 28-day extension by stipulation; more by motion. Custody/guardianship: 28 days.","c":"MCR 7.212(A)(1)(a)(iii)","due":{"n":56,"from":"the trigger date (latest of claim of appeal, certified leave order, transcript filing, or settled statement)","note":"One 28-day extension by stipulation; more by motion. Custody/guardianship: 28 days."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief (ordinary civil)","d":"35 days from service of appellant’s brief.\nOne 28-day extension by stipulation.","c":"MCR 7.212(A)(2)(a)(ii)","due":{"n":35,"from":"the trigger date (service of appellant’s brief)","note":"One 28-day extension by stipulation."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"21 days from service of appellee’s brief.\n3,200 words.","c":"MCR 7.212(G)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (service of appellee’s brief)","note":"3,200 words."}},{"t":"Motion for reconsideration (Court of Appeals)","d":"21 days from date of the order or date stamped on the opinion.\n3,200 words; late motions not accepted.","c":"MCR 7.215(I)(1)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (date of the order or date stamped on the opinion)","note":"3,200 words; late motions not accepted."}},{"t":"Request to publish an opinion","d":"21 days from release of the unpublished opinion (or denial of rehearing).","c":"MCR 7.215(D)(1)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (release of the unpublished opinion)","note":""}},{"t":"Supreme Court application (civil)","d":"42 days from court of Appeals decision, order denying timely reconsideration, or grant of publication.\n56 days in criminal cases.","c":"MCR 7.305(C)(2)","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (court of Appeals decision, order denying timely reconsideration, or grant of publication)","note":"56 days in criminal cases."}},{"t":"Supreme Court application from interlocutory Court of Appeals order","d":"28 days from the Court of Appeals order.","c":"MCR 7.305(C)(3)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (the Court of Appeals order)","note":""}},{"t":"Supreme Court bypass application","d":"42 days from filing of claim, application, or original action in the Court of Appeals.","c":"MCR 7.305(C)(1)","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (filing of claim, application, or original action in the Court of Appeals)","note":""}},{"t":"Answer to Supreme Court application","d":"28 days from service of the application.\nReply within 21 days after service of the answer (MCR 7.305(E)).","c":"MCR 7.305(D)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (service of the application)","note":"Reply within 21 days after service of the answer (MCR 7.305(E))."}},{"t":"Supreme Court motion for rehearing","d":"21 days from filing of the opinion.\n16,000 words.","c":"MCR 7.311(F)(1)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the opinion)","note":"16,000 words."}},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP (UPEPA) appeal by moving party","d":"21 days from entry of the order on the special motion.\nAppeal of right; all proceedings stayed.","c":"MCL 691.1859; MCR 2.114(E)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order on the special motion)","note":"Appeal of right; all proceedings stayed."}},{"t":"Appeal of right to circuit court (from district/probate court or agency)","d":"21 days from entry of the judgment, order, or decision, or order deciding a timely postjudgment motion.\nJurisdictional; unless a statute sets another time.","c":"MCR 7.104(A)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment, order, or decision, or order deciding a timely postjudgment motion)","note":"Jurisdictional; unless a statute sets another time."}}]]],"dl":[["Claim of appeal (civil appeal of right)",21,"Entry of the final judgment or order, or entry of an order deciding a timely postjudgment motion","MCR 7.204(A)(1)","Jurisdictional. File in the Court of Appeals with the entry fee."],["Application for leave to appeal (Court of Appeals)",21,"Entry of the order or judgment, or of an order deciding a timely postjudgment motion","MCR 7.205(A)(1)","Jurisdictional."],["Delayed application for leave (civil)","6 months","Entry of the judgment or order","MCR 7.205(A)(4)(a)","Discretionary; explain the delay."],["Delayed application after dismissal of a timely claim for lack of jurisdiction",21,"Entry of the dismissal order or order denying reconsideration","MCR 7.205(A)(4)(b)",""],["Tolling postjudgment motion (new trial, reconsideration, relief)",21,"Entry of judgment","MCR 7.204(A)(1)(d); MCR 2.611(B); MCR 2.119(F)(1)","Must be filed within the initial 21-day period to toll."],["Late-served judgment: claim of appeal",14,"Service of the judgment (if served late under MCR 2.602)","MCR 7.204(A)(3)","Requires an affidavit showing late service."],["Appellee appearance",14,"Service of the claim of appeal","MCR 7.204(G)","Optional, but needed to receive notice."],["Docketing statement",28,"Filing of the claim of appeal (or cross-appeal, or certification of order granting leave)","MCR 7.204(H); MCR 7.205(E)(3)","Civil appeals only."],["Claim of cross-appeal",21,"Later of filing or service of the claim of appeal; or certification of order granting leave","MCR 7.207(B)",""],["Answer to application for leave (Court of Appeals)",21,"Service of the application","MCR 7.205(C)",""],["Appellant’s brief (ordinary civil)",56,"Latest of claim of appeal, certified leave order, transcript filing, or settled statement","MCR 7.212(A)(1)(a)(iii)","One 28-day extension by stipulation; more by motion. Custody/guardianship: 28 days."],["Appellee’s brief (ordinary civil)",35,"Service of appellant’s brief","MCR 7.212(A)(2)(a)(ii)","One 28-day extension by stipulation."],["Reply brief",21,"Service of appellee’s brief","MCR 7.212(G)","3,200 words."],["Motion for reconsideration (Court of Appeals)",21,"Date of the order or date stamped on the opinion","MCR 7.215(I)(1)","3,200 words; late motions not accepted."],["Request to publish an opinion",21,"Release of the unpublished opinion (or denial of rehearing)","MCR 7.215(D)(1)",""],["Supreme Court application (civil)",42,"Court of Appeals decision, order denying timely reconsideration, or grant of publication","MCR 7.305(C)(2)","56 days in criminal cases."],["Supreme Court application from interlocutory Court of Appeals order",28,"The Court of Appeals order","MCR 7.305(C)(3)",""],["Supreme Court bypass application",42,"Filing of claim, application, or original action in the Court of Appeals","MCR 7.305(C)(1)",""],["Answer to Supreme Court application",28,"Service of the application","MCR 7.305(D)","Reply within 21 days after service of the answer (MCR 7.305(E))."],["Supreme Court motion for rehearing",21,"Filing of the opinion","MCR 7.311(F)(1)","16,000 words."],["Anti-SLAPP (UPEPA) appeal by moving party",21,"Entry of the order on the special motion","MCL 691.1859; MCR 2.114(E)","Appeal of right; all proceedings stayed."],["Appeal of right to circuit court (from district/probate court or agency)",21,"Entry of the judgment, order, or decision, or order deciding a timely postjudgment motion","MCR 7.104(A)","Jurisdictional; unless a statute sets another time."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.courts.michigan.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Michigan Court Rules of 1985","rulesSrc":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"2.001","t":"Applicability","s":"The Chapter 2 rules govern procedure in all civil proceedings in every Michigan court, except where a court’s limited jurisdiction makes a rule inherently inapplicable or a rule for a specific court or proceeding provides a different procedure.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.002","t":"Waiver of Fees for Indigent Persons","s":"Only individuals (not organizations, unless a statute says otherwise) may request a fee waiver, on a nonpublic SCAO “Fee Waiver Request” form filed in each case; prisoners must also file a certified institutional account with a 12-month deposit and withdrawal history. The clerk must waive fees for recipients of means-tested public assistance or clients of qualifying legal services programs or law school clinics, and the court must waive fees for households under 125% of the federal poverty guidelines (or above that on a showing of financial hardship), ruling within 3 business days. After a denial the individual must pay within 14 days of the clerk’s notice (prisoners: 21 days after the order) or the filing is rejected, and may seek de novo review within 14 days (prisoners: 21 days), which stays the case and must be held within 14 days, with hearing notice at least 9 days ahead by mail or 7 days by delivery. A waiver ends on appeal (a new request may be filed in the appellate court), the court may reinstate fees when the reason for waiver ends, and courts must allow an entirely electronic waiver process.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.003","t":"Disqualification of Judge","s":"Applies to all judges, including Supreme Court justices; grounds include bias, a serious risk of actual bias under Caperton, an appearance of impropriety, personal knowledge of disputed facts, prior involvement as counsel, having been a partner of or lawyer for a party or member of a party’s law firm within the preceding 2 years, more than a de minimis economic interest, or a relative within the third degree involved in the case. In trial courts the motion, with an affidavit and all known grounds, must be filed within 14 days of discovering the grounds (forthwith if discovered within 14 days of trial); in the Court of Appeals within 14 days of disclosure of the panel assignment or discovery; in the Supreme Court with the application or within 28 days, with requests for review by the entire Court within 14 days of the individual justice’s decision. The challenged judge decides first, with de novo review by the chief judge or an SCAO-assigned judge on request; untimely motions may be granted for good cause, and all parties may waive disqualification in writing or on the record.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.004","t":"Incarcerated Parties","s":"In domestic relations and other child custody, guardianship, neglect, foster-care, or termination cases involving a party in Department of Corrections custody, the moving party must confirm the incarceration, serve the prisoner, and state the prison number and location in the filing. The court must then order that the prisoner be allowed to participate by noncollect, unmonitored telephone call or videoconference, serving the order at least 7 days before the hearing; relief may not be granted unless the prisoner was offered the chance to participate, except for immediate temporary action to protect the child, and sanctions may be imposed for concealing the case from the prisoner.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.101","t":"Form and Commencement of Action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action,” and it is commenced by filing a complaint with a court. This mirrors Fed. R. Civ. P. 2 and 3.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.102","t":"Summons; Expiration; Dismissal for Failure to Serve","s":"The clerk issues a summons on filing of the complaint, listing 11 required items including its last valid date. A summons expires 91 days after issuance, but within those 91 days the judge may, on a showing of due diligence, order a second summons for a definite period not exceeding 1 year from issuance; the 91 days are tolled while a motion challenging the summons or service is pending. On expiration the action is automatically deemed dismissed without prejudice as to unserved defendants, and a dismissal may be set aside only by stipulation or if service was timely made, proof is filed or excused, and the motion is filed within 28 days after notice of dismissal. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(m), dismissal is automatic upon expiration of the summons rather than following notice and a court order, and the rule does not apply to summary proceedings to recover possession of realty.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.103","t":"Process; Who May Serve","s":"Any legally competent adult who is not a party or an officer of a corporate party may serve process. Writs of restitution and process seizing property may be served only by a sheriff, deputy, bailiff, or appointed court officer (or state or local police where the state or municipality is a party), service in a governmental institution must be made by the person in charge or a designee, and process requiring arrest may be served only by a sheriff, deputy, police officer, or appointed court officer.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.104","t":"Process; Proof of Service","s":"Proof of service may be made by the recipient’s signed acknowledgment, by a certificate of a sheriff, qualifying deputy or bailiff, court officer, or attorney for service in Michigan, or by a verified statement of the facts of service, stating the manner, time, date, and place. Failure to file proof does not affect the validity of service, and proof of publication, posting, and mailing is governed by MCR 2.106(G).","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.105","t":"Process; Manner of Service","s":"Individuals may be served by personal delivery or by registered or certified mail, return receipt requested, delivery restricted to the addressee, with service complete when the defendant acknowledges receipt; substituted methods apply to nonresidents, minors, persons with guardians or conservators, and assumed-name businesses. The rule specifies service on partnerships, corporations (including by registered mail to the corporation and the state Corporation Division when there is no resident agent), associations, LLCs, public bodies, and authorized agents, and service on an insurer may be made by providing 2 summonses and the complaint to the state insurance regulator. If service cannot reasonably be made as provided, the court may order alternative service on a verified motion dated not more than 14 days before filing, and no such service may occur before the order. Michigan process has no territorial limit, an action may not be dismissed for improper service unless the defendant was not informed of the action within the time for service, and “registered mail” includes certified mail; unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e), restricted-delivery mail is an authorized method of serving individuals directly under the rule.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.106","t":"Notice by Posting or Publication","s":"Governs service by publication or posting under a court order obtained by motion under MCR 2.105(J); the order must state the court, parties, nature of the proceeding, where and when to answer, and the effect of failing to answer. Publication runs once a week for 3 consecutive weeks in a qualifying newspaper, and posting runs for 3 continuous weeks in the courthouse and 2 or more other public places, each with a copy mailed by registered mail, return receipt requested, to the last known address if ascertainable. A qualifying newspaper must have paying subscribers or 2 years of weekly publication, at least 1 year of weekly general circulation in the county (interruptions excused for acts of God, labor disputes, COVID-19, or the publisher’s military service up to 2 years if resumed within 6 months), an annual average of at least 25% news and editorial content, and print and website versions with notices also posted on the statewide newspaper association repository. Proof is by publisher’s affidavit and verified statements of posting and mailing.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.107","t":"Service and Filing of Pleadings and Other Documents","s":"Every party who has filed a pleading, appearance, or motion must be served with all later filed documents, generally through the party’s attorney, except for the original summons and complaint, contempt notices, postjudgment matters after the appeal period, and limited-appearance situations where both the party and the limited scope attorney are served. Service is by delivery, mail (complete on mailing), e-service under MCR 1.109, or alternative electronic service by e-mail, text, or alert by stipulation, with transmissions sent by 11:59 p.m. deemed served that day (or the next business day if sent on a weekend, holiday, or court-closed day). Proof of service must be filed at or before the related hearing, and until further order all service except case initiation must be electronic to the greatest extent possible, with e-mail not requiring the other party’s agreement. This largely parallels Fed. R. Civ. P. 5.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.108","t":"Time","s":"A defendant must answer or take other permitted action within 21 days after personal service in Michigan, or 28 days if served outside Michigan or by registered mail, and not less than 28 days after publication or posting is completed. Answers to counterclaims and cross-claims, optional replies, motions directed at a pleading needing no response, medical malpractice answers after notice of the affidavit or security under MCL 600.2912d, and responsive pleadings after denial of a pre-answer motion or service of a more definite statement are all due within 21 days; responses to amended or supplemental pleadings are due in the longer of the remaining time or 21 days. The court may extend time if asked before the deadline, or afterward on motion for excusable neglect. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(a), which sets a uniform 21-day answer period after service, Michigan allows 28 days for out-of-state or mail service.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.109","t":"Security for Costs","s":"On motion, the court may order a party asserting a claim to post a bond with surety, in an amount set in its discretion, covering costs and recoverable expenses in the trial and any appellate court. Security is excused for a party with a legitimate claim who shows by affidavit an inability to pay, and is never required of the United States, Michigan or its governmental units, or exempt officers suing officially; the court may order new or additional security if the party or surety leaves Michigan or the bond proves insufficient. The Federal Rules have no general counterpart.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.110","t":"Pleadings","s":"Pleadings are limited to a complaint, cross-claim, counterclaim, third-party complaint, an answer to any of those, and a reply to an answer, and a responsive pleading is required to each of those claims and to an answer demanding a reply. Counterclaims and cross-claims combined with an answer must be clearly designated, though the court may treat misdesignated claims or defenses as properly designated. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 7(a), which allows a reply only if the court orders one, Michigan permits a reply to an answer and requires one when the answer demands it.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.111","t":"General Rules of Pleading","s":"Allegations must be clear, concise, and direct, and alternative or inconsistent claims and defenses are allowed. A claim must state the facts relied on and a demand for judgment, stating a specific dollar amount only for a sum certain or a claim of $25,000 or less; otherwise no amount may be stated and the pleading must show the claim is within the court’s jurisdiction. Responses must admit, deny (stating the substance relied on), plead no contest, or plead lack of knowledge, and undenied allegations other than damages are admitted; defenses other than subject-matter jurisdiction and failure to state a claim are waived if not raised, and affirmative defenses must be stated under a separate heading. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 8, Michigan allows a “no contest” response and bars stating a specific amount for unliquidated claims over $25,000.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.112","t":"Pleading Special Matters","s":"Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while capacity, conditions of mind, and conditions precedent may be pleaded generally, though challenges to capacity and denials of conditions precedent must be specific; the rule also covers insurance policies, written instruments (execution admitted unless denied by affidavit), official acts, judgments, statutes, special damages, foreign and tribal law (notice by the close of discovery), Headlee Amendment actions, consumer debt complaints (creditor name, account number, and balance), and business court verification. In tort cases subject to MCL 600.2957 and 600.6304, notice of nonparty fault must be filed within 91 days after the party’s first responsive pleading, and a served party may add the nonparty by amendment within 91 days of the first notice. In medical malpractice actions filed on or after October 1, 1993, affidavits under MCL 600.2912d and 600.2912e are required, challenges to a notice of intent must be made with the defendant’s first response, and challenges to an affidavit of merit or meritorious defense must be filed within 63 days of its service. This parallels Fed. R. Civ. P. 9 but adds many Michigan-specific requirements.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.113","t":"Form, Captioning, Signing, and Verifying of Documents","s":"Form, captions, signing, and verification are governed by MCR 1.109(D) and (E). Allegations must be in numbered paragraphs limited to a single set of circumstances, separate claims and defenses must be in separate counts, and a written instrument underlying a claim or defense must be attached unless it is of public record in the county, held by the adverse party, inaccessible, or impractical to attach, as stated in the pleading. Statements may be adopted by reference only within the same pleading, which is narrower than Fed. R. Civ. P. 10(c).","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.114","t":"Special Motions Under the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act","s":"Governs special motions to dismiss eligible claims under the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (MCL 691.1853 et seq.), which must be filed within 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim (extendable for good cause). Filing stays all proceedings between the movant and responding party, including discovery, until the ruling and the time to appeal expire (with limited discovery allowed on a showing of need), and an appeal stays the whole action. The court must hold a hearing within 60 days after filing (or within 60 days after ordering limited discovery, absent good cause) and rule within 60 days after the hearing, dismissing with prejudice if the claim is eligible and the responding party fails to make a prima facie case, fails to state a claim, or there is no genuine issue of material fact. The movant may appeal as of right under MCR 7.204, and costs, attorney fees, and expenses must be awarded to a prevailing movant (or to a prevailing respondent if the motion was frivolous or filed solely for delay); the Federal Rules have no counterpart.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.115","t":"Motion to Correct or to Strike Pleadings","s":"Before responding, a party may move for a more definite statement of a vague or ambiguous pleading, and if the order is not obeyed within 14 days after notice (or another court-set time), the court may strike the pleading or enter another just order. The court may strike redundant, immaterial, impertinent, scandalous, or indecent matter, or a nonconforming pleading, on motion or on its own. This parallels Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) and (f).","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.116","t":"Summary Disposition","s":"Parties may submit stipulated facts for judgment, and a party may move for dismissal or judgment on all or part of a claim or defense on 10 grounds: (C)(1) no personal jurisdiction, (2) insufficient process, (3) insufficient service, (4) no subject-matter jurisdiction, (5) lack of capacity, (6) another pending action on the same claim, (7) release, payment, prior judgment, immunity, limitations, statute of frauds, an arbitration or forum agreement, disability, or prior assignment, (8) failure to state a claim, (9) failure to state a valid defense, and (10) no genuine issue of material fact. Grounds (C)(1)-(3) are waived if not raised in the first motion or responsive pleading, (C)(5)-(7) must be raised in the responsive pleading or a prior motion, (C)(4) and governmental immunity may be raised any time, and (C)(8)-(10) any time unless a scheduling order deadline applies; a claimant’s motion may not be heard until at least 28 days after the opponent was served with the claim. Unless the court orders otherwise, the motion, brief, and affidavits must be filed and served at least 21 days before the hearing, the response at least 7 days before, and an optional reply limited to 5 pages at least 4 days before, with no further briefs without leave; (C)(8) and (9) motions are decided on the pleadings alone, while (C)(10) motions require evidence that would be admissible and the opponent must set out specific facts. The court may grant judgment to the nonmovant, order an immediate trial, and must allow amendment under MCR 2.118 on (C)(8)-(10) grounds unless unjustified; unlike the Federal Rules, which split these defenses between Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b) and 56, Michigan consolidates them in one motion.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.117","t":"Appearances","s":"A party appears by filing a notice or appearing in court (then promptly filing a written appearance), and an appearance alone neither confers nor enlarges jurisdiction but entitles the party to service. An attorney’s appearance counts as the party’s, a law firm filing is deemed the appearance of the first attorney filing and of every firm member, and an attorney may file a limited appearance under MRPC 1.2(b) defining its scope by date, time, or subject; court-appointed counsel for a whole case must file an appearance, but counsel appointed for a single hearing need not. An appearance lasts until final judgment and the appeal-of-right period passes, withdrawal otherwise requires a court order, and a limited-scope attorney may withdraw by notice that is effective immediately if signed by the client or 14 days after filing and service unless the client objects; an attorney who only ghostwrites documents has not appeared.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.118","t":"Amended and Supplemental Pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as of course within 14 days after being served with a responsive pleading, or within 14 days after serving a pleading that needs no response; otherwise amendment requires leave, freely given when justice requires, or the adverse party’s written consent, and the court may condition leave on reimbursement of expenses caused by inexcusable delay. A party must respond to an amended pleading within the MCR 2.108 time or file a notice that its earlier response stands; amendments to conform to the evidence are allowed, and an amendment relates back if it arises from the same conduct, transaction, or occurrence (medical malpractice affidavit amendments relate back to the original filing). The court may permit supplemental pleadings on motion. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(a)(1), which allows 21 days to amend as of course, Michigan allows 14 days.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.119","t":"Motion Practice","s":"Motions must be in writing, state their grounds, authority, and relief, and be signed; a motion or response raising an issue of law needs a brief, the combined motion and brief (or response and brief) may not exceed 20 double-spaced pages excluding exhibits, quotations and footnotes may be single-spaced, with at least 1-inch margins and type no smaller than 12-point, and reply or supplemental briefs are barred unless permitted. Unless the rules or the court set otherwise, a motion must be served at least 9 days before the hearing by mail or 7 days by delivery or e-service and filed at least 7 days before, and a response served at least 5 days before by mail or 3 days by delivery and filed at least 3 days before. A proposed order served for stipulation is deemed rejected unless stipulated or notice and hearing are waived within 7 days, a moving party who fails to appear faces costs and a penalty of up to $100, and a motion for rehearing or reconsideration must be filed within 21 days after the order and must show palpable error that would change the result, with no response or argument unless ordered. A single motion fee covers all motions filed together, and no fee is charged in criminal cases, for notices of settlement of orders, fee waiver requests, motions filed with another fee-bearing document, or uncontested orders; unlike the Federal Rules, Michigan sets page limits, a stipulated-order procedure, and a reconsideration standard in the rule itself.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.201","t":"Parties Plaintiff and Defendant; Capacity","s":"Actions must be brought by the real party in interest, though representatives, trustees, contracting parties for another’s benefit, and statutorily authorized persons may sue in their own names, and suits to stop illegal state spending may be brought by a civic nonprofit or by at least 5 Michigan residents who own taxable property in their county. The rule sets the capacity of individuals, assumed-name businesses, partnerships and associations, corporations, and governmental units and officers, and allows unknown claimants, owners, or heirs to be named by description in a verified complaint. A minor or incompetent person is represented by a conservator or, if none, a court-appointed next friend (as plaintiff) or guardian ad litem (as defendant), nominated by the minor if 14 or older or by next of kin if younger or incompetent, or appointed on motion if no nomination is made or approved within 21 days after service.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.202","t":"Substitution of Parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, the court may order substitution, but unless a motion is made within 91 days after filing and service of a statement noting the death, the action must be dismissed as to the deceased party unless later substitution would not prejudice anyone. Actions continue on transfer of interest unless the court orders substitution or joinder, actions involving public officers continue against successors without a formal order, and substitution may be ordered at any stage, including on appeal. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 25(a), which allows 90 days, Michigan allows 91 days and permits later substitution absent prejudice.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.203","t":"Joinder of Claims, Counterclaims, and Cross-Claims","s":"A pleader must join every claim it has against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence, unless adjudication would require third parties beyond the court’s jurisdiction, and may join any other claims. Counterclaims may exceed or differ from the opposing claim, cross-claims against co-parties must arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or property, and both must be filed with the answer or by amendment under MCR 2.118. New parties may be added to a counterclaim or cross-claim, and their summons is valid for 21 days unless the court orders otherwise. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a) and 18, Michigan’s compulsory joinder applies to all claims a pleader has against an opposing party, not only counterclaims.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.204","t":"Third-Party Practice","s":"Subject to MCL 500.3030, a defending party may bring in a nonparty who may be liable for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 21 days after its original answer, and otherwise by leave on motion with notice; the third-party summons is valid for 21 days unless the court orders otherwise. The third-party defendant answers within the MCR 2.108(A)(1)-(3) times, may assert the defendant’s defenses and claims against the plaintiff, and the plaintiff may assert related claims against it; plaintiffs facing counterclaims may also implead, and the rule does not apply in small claims. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 14(a)(1), which allows impleader without leave within 14 days after the original answer, Michigan allows 21 days and limits the summons to 21 days.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.205","t":"Necessary Joinder of Parties","s":"Subject to MCR 3.501, persons whose presence is essential for complete relief must be joined and aligned according to their interests, and if subject to jurisdiction the court must order them summoned. If they can be brought in only by consent, the court may proceed after weighing whether a valid judgment can be rendered without them, whether the plaintiff has another remedy, the prejudice to the defendant or absent person, and whether protective provisions can reduce it; pleaders must name omitted necessary persons and explain why they are not joined. This parallels Fed. R. Civ. P. 19.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.206","t":"Permissive Joinder of Parties","s":"Persons may join as plaintiffs or be joined as defendants if the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact, or if their presence will promote the convenient administration of justice; parties need not be interested in all relief, and judgment may be rendered as their rights are determined. The court may order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, or expense. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 20(a), Michigan also allows joinder solely because it promotes the convenient administration of justice.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.207","t":"Misjoinder and Nonjoinder of Parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped by court order on motion or on the court’s own initiative at any stage on just terms, and claims may be severed. When additional persons are needed for complete relief on a counterclaim or cross-claim, the court must order them brought in as defendants if jurisdiction can be obtained. This parallels Fed. R. Civ. P. 21.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.209","t":"Intervention","s":"On timely application a person may intervene as of right when a Michigan statute or rule confers an unconditional right, when all parties stipulate, or when the person has an interest that the action may impair and that existing parties do not adequately represent; permissive intervention is allowed when a statute or rule confers a conditional right or a common question of law or fact exists, considering undue delay or prejudice. The motion must state the grounds, be served on all parties under MCR 2.107, and be accompanied by a pleading, and the court may require notice to the Attorney General when the validity of a Michigan statute or administrative rule is challenged and the state is not a party. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 24, Michigan grants intervention of right by stipulation of all parties.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.221","t":"Motion for Change of Venue","s":"A motion for change of venue must be filed before or with the defendant’s answer, and an objection not timely raised is waived. A later motion may not be denied as untimely if the facts supporting it were not and could not with reasonable diligence have been known more than 14 days before the motion was filed.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.222","t":"Change of Venue; Venue Proper","s":"When venue is proper, the court may change it only on a party’s motion, for the convenience of parties and witnesses or when an impartial trial cannot be had (and, for appellate review of agency proceedings, for attorneys’ convenience), and may separate and transfer improperly joined claims or retain the whole action. The moving party must pay the receiving court’s filing fee (unless waived under MCR 2.002) within 28 days of the transfer order, or the case is transferred back; any paid jury fee is forwarded to the receiving court.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.223","t":"Change of Venue; Venue Improper","s":"If venue is improper, the court must change it on a defendant’s timely motion, or may do so on its own after notice and an opportunity to be heard, and may transfer only to a county where venue is proper. The plaintiff must pay the filing fee and any compensation and attorney fees ordered for filing in the wrong court within 28 days of the transfer order or the receiving court will dismiss the action, and no proceedings occur until payment; MCL 600.1653 applies to tort actions filed on or after October 1, 1986.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.224","t":"Change of Venue in Tort Actions (Repealed)","s":"Repealed November 6, 1996, effective February 1, 1997.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.225","t":"Joinder of Party to Control Venue","s":"On a defendant’s motion, venue must be changed if it is proper only because a codefendant was joined in bad faith to control venue. The plaintiff must pay the receiving court’s filing fee and any ordered expenses or attorney fees within 28 days of the transfer order or the receiving court will dismiss the action.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.226","t":"Change of Venue; Transfer of Jurisdiction; Orders","s":"A court changing venue or transferring jurisdiction must enter its orders on an SCAO-approved form. If the order lacks the information needed to identify the rule under which transfer was ordered, the receiving clerk must refuse the transfer and return the case within 7 business days of receipt, and the transferring court must issue a proper order and retransfer within 7 business days.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.227","t":"Transfer of Actions on Finding of Lack of Jurisdiction","s":"A court lacking subject-matter jurisdiction may transfer the action to a Michigan court that has jurisdiction where venue is proper, after notice and an opportunity to be heard if the court raises the issue itself; a circuit court may transfer to district court based on amount in controversy only on a good-faith stipulation or a legal certainty that the amount does not exceed the district court limit. The plaintiff must pay the filing fee and prove payment of ordered expenses within 28 days of the transfer order or the receiving court will dismiss, and the case then proceeds as if originally filed there, with pleading deadlines running from fee payment and a new summons needed for unserved defendants. A prior jury waiver is ineffective after transfer, and a party may demand a jury by paying the jury fee within 28 days after the filing fee is paid, while a prior jury demand is preserved.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.228","t":"Transfer to the Court of Claims","s":"A notice of transfer to the Court of Claims must be filed before or with the defendant’s answer. Afterward, if the court has concurrent jurisdiction with the Court of Claims, the defendant needs leave, which may be granted if the supporting facts were not and could not with reasonable diligence have been known more than 14 days before the motion; if the Court of Claims has exclusive jurisdiction, MCR 2.227 governs.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.301","t":"Availability and Timing of Discovery","s":"Where initial disclosures are required, a party may seek discovery only after serving its own initial disclosures; in district court, discovery before judgment requires leave of court or stipulation of all parties, and none is allowed in small claims or civil infraction actions. In circuit and probate court the discovery cutoff is set by scheduling order, and requests must be served early enough that the response is due before the cutoff, though preservation depositions may be taken after the cutoff without leave. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(d), the start of discovery is tied to serving initial disclosures rather than to a parties’ discovery conference.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.302","t":"Duty to Disclose; General Rules Governing Discovery","s":"Without a request, a party must disclose the factual basis and legal theories of its claims and defenses, likely witnesses, documents and ESI, a damages computation, insurance agreements, and the subject areas of expected expert testimony, with extra disclosures in no-fault and personal-injury cases and 10 listed categories of exempt cases. Claimants must serve disclosures within 14 days after an opposing party answers; answering parties within the later of 14 days after the opposing disclosures are due or 28 days after answering; later-appearing or later-joined parties must be served or must serve within 14 days. Discovery covers nonprivileged matter relevant and proportional to the needs of the case, with protections for trial-preparation material, expert drafts and attorney-expert communications, inadvertently produced privileged material, and protective orders; parties must supplement incomplete or incorrect disclosures and responses, and improper certifications can draw sanctions including attorney fees. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(1), the Michigan disclosures include the legal theories and the expected subject areas of expert testimony, and the deadlines run from the answer rather than from a discovery-planning conference.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.303","t":"Depositions Before Action or Pending Appeal","s":"A person expecting to be a party to a Michigan action may petition the circuit court of the county where an expected adverse party lives to take depositions to perpetuate testimony, serving notice at least 21 days before the hearing in the manner used for a summons. While an appeal is pending, or before one is taken while appeal time remains, the trial court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony for possible further proceedings.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.304","t":"Persons Before Whom Depositions May Be Taken","s":"In the United States, depositions may be taken before anyone authorized to give oaths, a court-appointed person, or a person the parties stipulate to; abroad, they may be taken on notice before an authorized person, before a court-commissioned person, or under a letter rogatory. Unless the parties agree otherwise, the officer may not be a relative, employee, or attorney of a party, a relative or employee of a party’s attorney, or someone financially interested in the action.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.305","t":"Discovery Subpoena to a Non-Party","s":"A represented party may subpoena a non-party for a deposition, documents, things, or entry on land once all parties have had a reasonable time to get counsel (or on court order), and an unrepresented party must ask the court to issue one; the subpoena must allow at least 14 days to comply unless the court shortens it, and a timely motion to quash stays compliance. A subpoena naming an organization as deponent must be served at least 14 days before the deposition, the entity may object or seek a protective order within 10 days, and each designated witness’s deposition is limited to one day of seven hours. Compliance can be required only in the county where the non-party lives, works, or does business, at the location of the things or land, or at a court-ordered place, and the subpoena may require appearance by phone or video.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.306","t":"Depositions on Oral Examination of a Party","s":"A plaintiff needs leave of court to depose before the defendant has had a reasonable time to get a lawyer, which is deemed to have passed once the defendant answers, appears, seeks discovery, files an MCR 2.116 motion, or 28 days have run after service; depositions of prisoners and state institution patients need leave, and any deposition is limited to one day of seven hours. Notice to an organizational deponent must be served at least 14 days ahead and objections are due within 10 days after service; objections must be concise and nonsuggestive, instructions not to answer are limited, and the deponent may not communicate (including by text or email) while a question is pending except about privilege. The court may sanction anyone who impedes the examination, privilege must be asserted at the deposition or is lost for the action, and a party who fails to attend its own noticed deposition or to subpoena its witness may have to pay others’ expenses and attorney fees.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.307","t":"Depositions on Written Questions","s":"A party may depose any person, including a party or an organization, on written questions served on all parties with a notice. Cross-questions are due within 14 days after service of the notice and questions, redirect questions within 7 days after cross-questions, and recross-questions within 7 days after redirect, unless the parties stipulate in writing or the court changes the times.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.308","t":"Use of Depositions in Court Proceedings","s":"Depositions are admissible at trial, at motion hearings, or in interlocutory proceedings only as the Michigan Rules of Evidence allow. Errors in the notice, the officer’s qualifications, and curable defects in the manner of taking are waived unless promptly raised, objections to the form of written questions are waived unless served within the time for the next round of questions and within 7 days after the last questions, and no error bars use unless it substantially destroys the deposition’s value or makes its use unfair. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 32, the Michigan rule does not itself list when a deposition may be used and instead defers to the rules of evidence.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.309","t":"Interrogatories to Parties","s":"Each separately represented party may serve no more than 20 interrogatories on each party, with each discrete subpart counted separately, and answers must be given separately and fully under oath. Answers and objections are due within 28 days after service, or for a defendant within 42 days after service of the summons and complaint, and a motion to compel must state that the movant conferred or tried to confer in good faith; a party may answer by specifying business records when the burden of finding the answer is substantially the same for both sides. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 33, which allows 25 interrogatories and 30 days to respond, Michigan allows 20 and 28 days.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.310","t":"Requests for Production of Documents and Other Things; Entry on Land","s":"A party may request that another party produce documents, ESI, and tangible things or permit entry on land, and may make similar requests of a nonparty (through a subpoena under MCR 2.305). The written response is due within 28 days after service, or for a defendant within 42 days after service of the summons and complaint; if no ESI form is specified it must be produced as ordinarily kept or in a reasonably usable form, and only in one form. Unless the court orders otherwise for good cause, the producing party bears the cost of assembling items and the requesting party pays copying costs. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 34, the response time is 28 days rather than 30.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.311","t":"Physical and Mental Examination of Persons","s":"When a party’s (or a person in a party’s custody’s) physical or mental condition, including blood group, is in controversy, the court may order an examination on motion for good cause with notice, specifying its time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. On request the order may let the examinee’s attorney attend or require a mental examination to be video- or audio-recorded, and any recording must be unobtrusive, capture both participants throughout, and be filed under seal; the examinee may obtain the examiner’s detailed report but then waives privilege as to others who examined the same condition. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 35, the Michigan rule expressly allows the order to permit the examinee’s attorney to be present and the examination to be recorded.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.312","t":"Request for Admission","s":"Within the discovery period, a party may serve requests to admit facts, the application of law to fact, or the genuineness of documents, each clearly labeled as a Request for Admission. A matter is deemed admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 28 days after service, or for a defendant within 42 days after service of the summons and complaint (unless the court sets another time), and an admission is conclusive in that action only unless the court allows withdrawal for good cause. Requests and responses must be filed with the court before or within a reasonable time after service. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 36, the response time is 28 days rather than 30 and the papers must be filed with the court.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.313","t":"Failure to Serve Disclosure or to Provide or to Permit Discovery; Sanctions","s":"A party may move to compel initial disclosures, deposition answers, entity designations, interrogatory answers, inspection, attendance, or non-party subpoena compliance; evasive or incomplete responses count as failures, and the losing side may be ordered to pay reasonable expenses including attorney fees. Disobeying a discovery order can lead to facts being taken as established, exclusion of evidence, striking pleadings, dismissal, default judgment, contempt (except for an order to submit to examination), and expense awards. A party that fails to disclose or supplement may not use the information or witness unless the failure was substantially justified or harmless, a party who wrongly refuses an admission may have to pay the cost of proving the matter, and lost ESI can bring curative measures or, only on a finding of intent to deprive, an adverse presumption, adverse-inference instruction, dismissal, or default.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.314","t":"Discovery of Medical Information Concerning Party","s":"When a party’s mental or physical condition is in controversy, medical information about it is discoverable unless the party asserts a valid privilege, which must be raised in the initial disclosures, a written discovery response, at or before a deposition, or by protective-order motion, or it is waived for that action; a party who uses privilege to block discoverable medical information may not later offer evidence of the condition unless the court orders otherwise. A party served with a request may produce the records, assert privilege, object, or supply signed SCAO-approved authorizations and the records’ location, and a custodian must comply with an authorized request within 28 days after receipt (or 28 days after the patient’s discharge if hospitalized for the condition) and may require advance payment of reasonable expenses. Medical information about nonparties is not discoverable under this rule.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.315","t":"Video Depositions","s":"Depositions under MCR 2.303 and 2.306 may be video-recorded without leave or stipulation if the notice and subpoena say so, a digital clock showing hours, minutes, and seconds stays in the picture, and the opening, oath, and closing are made on camera. The recording must be sealed and kept by the operator, may be filed only on a party’s request with an affidavit of elapsed time and no alterations, and cannot be e-filed; it may be used in court only after filing and with either a transcript for ruling on objections or a stipulation that there are no objections. Costs may be taxed under MCR 2.625 like other depositions.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.316","t":"Removal of Disclosure and Discovery Materials From File","s":"Disclosure and discovery materials in civil court files may be removed and destroyed by stipulation, by court order on a finding they are no longer needed, or by the clerk. The clerk may start removal 18 months after judgment or dismissal if no appeal is taken, or 91 days after appellate proceedings end (unless remanded), giving at least 28 days’ notice during which the filing party may retrieve the materials or any party may object, which requires a hearing. No materials may be destroyed until those 18-month or 91-day periods have passed.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.401","t":"Pretrial Procedures; Conferences; Scheduling Orders","s":"The court may call conferences at any time and may hold an early scheduling conference covering topics such as ADR, ESI, disclosure timing, expert limits, and settlement, then enter a scheduling order setting dates for ADR, amendments, motions, disclosures, discovery limits, discovery completion, witness lists, and pretrial or trial; if the order was entered without meaningful consultation, a party may request amendment within 14 days and the court must act within 14 days of the request. On court order or a party’s written request the parties must confer on a discovery plan, and failure to participate in good faith may draw sanctions including attorney fees and costs; the court may require parties or insurers with settlement authority to attend, and nonattendance may result in default or dismissal unless that would cause manifest injustice or the failure was not culpably negligent. The court may hold a final pretrial conference and order a joint final pretrial order, may bar unlisted witnesses except on good cause, and after an ESI conference the parties must file an ESI discovery plan within 14 days. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(f), the parties’ discovery-planning conference is required only on court order or another party’s written request.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.402","t":"Use of Communication Equipment","s":"The court may direct that conference telephones or similar audio equipment (not an audio-only connection to a video platform) be used for motion hearings and pretrial, scheduling, or status conferences, and with all parties’ consent or for good cause may take witness testimony that way, with a verbatim record still required. A party wanting to use such equipment must file a written request at least 7 days in advance, serve it, and send a copy to the judge’s office unless good cause excuses this. The requesting party pays the cost unless the court directs otherwise, and costs are shared equally when the court initiates use.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.403","t":"Case Evaluation","s":"The court may send any civil action seeking primarily money damages or property division to case evaluation unless the parties stipulate to another ADR process, which must finish within 60 days after discovery closes and, if there is no discovery plan, be submitted within 120 days of the first responsive pleading; a motion to remove a case must be filed within 14 days after notice of the referral and heard within 14 days after filing. A 3-person panel hears the case after at least 42 days’ notice; each party pays a single $75 fee ($50 if a judge is on the panel), with refunds if the parties report settlement or similar disposition at least 14 days before the hearing, and summaries (up to 20 double-spaced pages, at least 12-point font) are due at least 7 days before the hearing, with a $150 penalty for each late filing and an additional $150 for filing within 24 hours of the hearing. Oral presentations are limited to 15 minutes per side, the panel’s evaluation is due within 7 days (served within 14 days if not given at the hearing), and each party has 28 days after service to accept or reject, with silence counting as rejection; if all accept, judgment enters unless the award is paid within 28 days, in which case the action is dismissed with prejudice. A party whose claim or defense the panel unanimously finds frivolous in certain tort cases may seek court review within 14 days, and otherwise must post a $5,000 bond per opposing party within 56 days after the hearing or at least 14 days before trial, whichever is earlier, or face dismissal or default; the rule as printed contains no cost or fee sanction for simply rejecting an evaluation, and there is no Federal Rule counterpart.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.404","t":"Selection of Case Evaluation Panels","s":"Each court using case evaluation must adopt a written selection plan by local administrative order and keep a public list of evaluators, who must have practiced law at least 5 years, be State Bar members in good standing, practice locally, and have devoted a substantial part of the last 5 years to civil litigation (at least 3 years in the specialty for specialized sublists). Applications are reviewed at least annually by a court employee or a committee of at least 3 members (no member may serve more than 3 years in any 9-year period), listings last no more than 7 years before reapplication, and neutral evaluators may be selected based on up to 15 years of representing both sides or ADR service. The ADR clerk assigns evaluators randomly or by rotation, using one plaintiff-side, one defense-side, and one neutral evaluator when sublists exist, and the chief judge reviews the plan at least annually to keep the list reflective of the bar’s racial, ethnic, and gender diversity.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.405","t":"Offers to Stipulate to Entry of Judgment","s":"Until 28 days before trial, any party may serve a written offer to stipulate to judgment for a sum certain (deemed to include accrued costs and interest); the offeree has 21 days to accept or make a counteroffer, and failure to accept within 21 days is a rejection. If the adjusted verdict (verdict plus interest and costs through the offer date) is more favorable to the offeror than the average of the offer and counteroffer, the offeree must pay the offeror’s actual costs, meaning taxable costs plus a reasonable attorney fee from the rejection onward; the reverse applies if it is more favorable to the offeree, but an offeree who made no counteroffer recovers only if the offer was made less than 42 days before trial. The court may refuse an attorney fee in the interest of justice, for example for token offers or issues of first impression or public interest, a request for costs must be filed within 28 days after judgment or an order denying a timely post-trial motion, and the rule does not apply to class actions. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 68, which only defending parties may use and which shifts only costs, any party may make a Michigan offer and the penalty includes reasonable attorney fees.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.406","t":"Use of Facsimile Communication Equipment","s":"Courts may allow documents to be filed by fax, but any filing fee must be paid or deposited in advance (except as MCR 2.002 provides), filings must be on 8½ by 11-inch paper in at least 12-point type, and courts may charge SCAO-scheduled fees and cap the number of pages. A fax received during regular business hours is filed that day if transmission begins during business hours and the whole document arrives, while later, weekend, or holiday receipts are filed the next business day; faxed documents count as originals and faxed signatures are valid.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.407","t":"Videoconferencing","s":"Videoconferenced proceedings, which include audio-only connections to a video platform, follow SCAO standards, and courts may require participants to appear by video, but a participant may ask to appear in person, in which case the judge and that participant’s attorney must appear in person too while others may still appear remotely. The court must weigh 12 listed factors (such as prejudice, cross-examination, fundamental interests at stake, and security) and state its reasons when requiring in-person appearance where video is presumed; it must give notice of how to join, allow private attorney-client communication, reschedule after technology failures, record proceedings verbatim, and give the public access. Remote participants must meet SCAO technical standards and provide confidential contact information, and there is no motion fee for a videoconferencing request.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.408","t":"Use of Videoconferencing Technology in Civil Cases","s":"A court may allow any participant to appear by video in any civil proceeding, but video may be used for trials or proceedings involving testimony or evidence only in the court’s discretion after notice and an opportunity to be heard. Video is presumed in circuit court for 10 listed proceedings, including civil pretrials, early scheduling conferences, discovery motions, adjournments, scheduling-order changes, motions in limine, postjudgment matters, certain MCR 3.210(A)(4) proofs, pleading motions, and MCR 2.116 motions, and in district court for 6 listed proceedings, unless the court finds video inappropriate under the MCR 2.407(C) factors.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.410","t":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","s":"All civil cases are subject to ADR (including settlement conferences, case evaluation, mediation, and other stipulated or local processes) unless a statute or rule says otherwise, and each court using ADR must adopt a written ADR plan that designates an ADR clerk and ensures access for indigent parties, who may not be ordered into a paid ADR process if free or low-cost services are unavailable. The court may order ADR at any time after consulting the parties, specifying the provider, time limits, and payment, and may require attendance by parties or representatives with settlement authority, with failure to attend potentially resulting in default or dismissal unless that would cause manifest injustice or the failure was not culpably negligent. A party may move to set aside or modify an ADR referral within 14 days after the order, and the chief judge must review the plan at least annually.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.411","t":"Mediation","s":"Parties may stipulate to any willing mediator, otherwise the court appoints one by rotation from its list, and the court may not influence the choice except on all parties’ request; mediators are disqualified on the same grounds as judges. The mediator must report completion to the court within 7 days, stating only the date, participants, whether the case settled, and whether further ADR is planned, and if the case settles the attorneys must submit closing papers within 21 days; fees are split pro rata unless agreed or ordered otherwise and are due by the earliest of 42 days after the mediation ends, judgment, or dismissal. Listed general civil mediators need approved training plus a law or conflict-resolution graduate degree or 40 hours of mediation experience over two years, must observe two mediations and conduct one under supervision, are listed for no more than 7 years, must complete 8 hours of advanced training every 2 years, and may seek the chief judge’s reconsideration within 21 days of rejection or removal. The parties may stipulate to, or the court may order, mediation of discovery disputes, including by an MRE 706 expert in complex ESI matters.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.412","t":"Mediation Communications; Confidentiality and Disclosure","s":"Mediation communications in cases referred under MCR 2.411 or 3.216 are confidential, not discoverable, not admissible, and may not be disclosed to non-participants except under 12 listed exceptions. These include written agreement of all parties, a statute or rule requiring disclosure, the mediator’s report, fee disputes, failure-to-attend issues, open sessions, program administration, threats or crimes, abuse or neglect reports, professional misconduct and malpractice claims, and (after an in camera hearing showing the evidence is otherwise unavailable and the need substantially outweighs confidentiality) proceedings over a signed or recorded mediated agreement. Only the portion needed for the exception may be disclosed, and otherwise admissible evidence does not become protected just because it was used in mediation.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.420","t":"Settlements and Judgments for Minors and Legally Incapacitated Individuals","s":"A consent judgment, settlement, or settlement dismissal in an action brought for a minor or legally incapacitated individual (or involving their share of a wrongful-death recovery) must be approved as fair by the assigned judge, and in injury claims the person must appear unless excused for good cause; a guardian ad litem must be appointed if the next friend, guardian, or conservator will share in the recovery. If a minor will receive more than $5,000 at once or more than $5,000 in any single year, a probate-court conservator must be appointed and the court must receive written verification that the conservator’s bond has been approved before entering judgment or dismissal; payments of $5,000 or less per year may be made under MCL 700.5102. Any trust created by the settlement may not be funded without prior probate-court approval after notice and a hearing.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.421","t":"Notice of Bankruptcy Proceedings","s":"A party who is a named debtor in a bankruptcy case must file notice of the bankruptcy in the state court action no later than 3 days after becoming subject to it and serve all other parties, and any other party may file and serve the notice if the debtor has not. The notice must give the debtor’s name, the bankruptcy court and case number, and, if available, the debtor’s bankruptcy attorney’s contact information. The court may hold the case in abeyance or administratively close it and may reopen it once the automatic stay no longer impairs adjudication.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.501","t":"Scheduling Trials; Court Calendars","s":"Unless a scheduling order already governs, the court must schedule a pretrial conference, ADR, or trial, or enter another order to prepare the case, and it may shorten the time to trial; child-custody contests and other cases given statutory or rule priority go first. Parties and attorneys get 28 days’ notice of trial assignments unless a rule or statute provides otherwise, the trial is an adjournment of an earlier date, or the court directs otherwise for good cause. When attorneys have conflicting trial dates, counsel must alert the courts promptly, and the case whose trial date was set first generally has priority.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.502","t":"Dismissal for Lack of Progress","s":"The court may give notice that a case with no steps taken within 91 days will be dismissed for lack of progress unless the parties show progress is being made or the delay is not the fault of the party seeking relief; no notice may issue while a scheduling order’s deadlines are running or the case is set for a conference, ADR, hearing, or trial. Such a dismissal is without prejudice unless the court says otherwise, and the court may reinstate the case on motion for good cause.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.503","t":"Adjournments","s":"A request to adjourn a trial, ADR process, pretrial conference, or motion hearing must be made by written or on-the-record motion or stipulation for good cause, stating who asks, why, and how many prior adjournments were granted, and its title must say whether it is the first or a later request. Adjournment for a missing witness or evidence requires materiality and diligent efforts, and the court may impose costs and conditions and must reschedule for a specific date or place the case on a list. If an attorney dies, is disabled, disbarred, suspended, made inactive, or resigns, the court must adjourn, and the party gets 28 days’ notice to obtain a new attorney or say it will self-represent.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.504","t":"Dismissal of Actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order, on payment of costs, by notice before the defendant serves an answer or an MCR 2.116 motion, or by stipulation signed by all parties; this is without prejudice unless stated otherwise, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal at the plaintiff’s request needs a court order, and the court may dismiss or default a party that violates the rules or a court order, or dismiss after the plaintiff’s evidence in a bench trial. Involuntary dismissals operate as adjudications on the merits unless the order says otherwise or the dismissal is for lack of jurisdiction or failure to join a party. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a), voluntary dismissal without an order is expressly conditioned on payment of costs.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.505","t":"Consolidation; Separate Trials","s":"When pending actions share a substantial and controlling common question of law or fact, the court may hold a joint hearing or trial, consolidate them, and enter orders to avoid unnecessary cost or delay. The court may order separate trials of claims or issues for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for expedition and economy. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 42(a), which requires only a common question, Michigan requires the common question to be substantial and controlling.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.506","t":"Subpoena; Order to Attend","s":"The court, or an attorney of record or the clerk, may subpoena a party or witness to testify at trial or a hearing and to produce documents or tangible things (including electronically stored information), and the recipient may file written objections before the appearance time. Unless the court orders otherwise, the subpoena must be served at least 2 days before the appearance, or 14 days before when documents are requested, with attendance and mileage fees tendered (or, for service by mail with acknowledgment card, paid when the witness appears); a party may be served under MCR 2.107 without fees. Electronic service on listed government agencies and labs requires a confirmation within 2 business days, and disobedience can be contempt or, for a party, lead to sanctions up to dismissal or default. Hospitals may comply by sending a certified copy of records to the clerk, which the clerk returns (if requested) 42 days after the final order or final appellate order, and otherwise may destroy.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.507","t":"Conduct of Trials","s":"The party opening the evidence (usually the plaintiff) makes the first opening statement and the adverse party follows, and the defendant goes first only if the answer admits the plaintiff’s case and the defendant bears the burden on a defense. Only one attorney per party may examine a given witness unless the court allows otherwise, the court sitting without a jury may view the scene, and the party who opened the evidence argues first and may rebut. Agreements between parties about the proceedings bind only if made in open court or in a writing signed by the party (or attorney) against whom offered.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.508","t":"Jury Trial of Right","s":"A party must file a written jury demand, as a separate document with the jury fee paid, within 28 days after the answer or a timely reply is filed; another party may demand jury trial of remaining issues within 14 days after service of a partial demand. A timely demand carries over when a whole case is removed or transferred between district and circuit court (paying any jury-fee difference when going to circuit court), but after a partial removal or transfer, or with no earlier demand, a new demand and fee are due within 28 days after the filing fee is paid in the receiving court and no later than 56 days after the removal or transfer order. Failing to demand or pay the fee waives a jury, and a demand cannot be withdrawn without all parties’ consent. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(b), which allows 14 days after service of the last pleading and a demand within a pleading, Michigan allows 28 days after the answer, requires a separate document, and requires payment of a jury fee.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.509","t":"Trial by Jury or by Court","s":"Issues on which a jury was properly demanded must be tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or the court finds no jury right; all other issues are tried by the court, which may in its discretion order a jury anyway. The court may set the order of trial of jury and nonjury issues while preserving the jury right, and in nonjury matters may use an advisory jury or, with all parties’ consent, a jury whose verdict is binding.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.510","t":"Juror Personal History Questionnaire","s":"Each juror drawn must complete a questionnaire adopted by the state court administrator before being called, and refusing to answer or answering falsely is contempt of court. Only the judges, the clerk’s office, the parties and their attorneys in the juror’s cases, and others authorized by rule or order may see it, attorneys must get a reasonable chance to review questionnaires before challenges for cause, and questionnaires must be kept for 3 years. The first summons to a juror must be written notice to the juror’s residence by ordinary mail or personal service, and a district covering a multicounty city draws jurors from the whole city.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.511","t":"Impaneling the Jury","s":"Prospective jurors are chosen by random blind draw or another fair method, and the court may examine them or let attorneys do so (allowing proper follow-up questions). The court may seat 7 or more jurors, but unless the parties stipulate that all may deliberate, names are drawn to reduce the jury to 6 before deliberations; retained alternates who later replace a juror require deliberations to start over. Challenges for cause may be made on 12 listed grounds, including bias, relation within the ninth degree to a party or attorney, and financial interest, and no one may be discriminated against in voir dire based on race, color, religion, national origin, or sex. Each party gets 3 peremptory challenges (parties on the same side count as one, but adverse co-parties with different attorneys get 3 each and the other side may receive up to the same total), exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff, with a pass waiving challenges to the panel as then constituted; jurors then take an oath and are instructed not to discuss, research, or use electronic devices to obtain information about the case. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(a), under which all seated jurors deliberate with no alternates, Michigan by default reduces the panel to 6 by lot.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.512","t":"Instructions to Jury","s":"Parties must file written instruction requests when the court directs, or otherwise by the close of evidence, and after the evidence each party must submit a written statement of the issues and may submit its theory of the case; the court must tell attorneys its rulings on requests before closing arguments. A party may claim instructional error only by objecting on the record before the jury retires (or before it resumes deliberations for later instructions), stating the matter and grounds specifically. Applicable, accurate, requested model civil jury instructions must be given, and the court may not give an instruction the committee recommends against unless it finds on the record that one is needed and not otherwise covered.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.513","t":"Conduct of Jury Trial","s":"After the jury is sworn the court must give oral and written preliminary instructions, including the elements of the claims and the burdens of proof, and must make all court–jury communications on the record with the parties notified. The court may allow juror notebooks, deposition summaries, sequenced expert testimony, juror note taking (notes destroyed after trial), juror questions to witnesses through the court, jury views, and, in civil cases, juror discussion of the evidence during recesses when all jurors are present. The plaintiff argues first and may rebut, the court may sum up the evidence without commenting on credibility, and the court must give final instructions in writing for the jury room, invite questions, answer every jury question, and not refuse reasonable requests to review testimony or evidence.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.514","t":"Rendering Verdict","s":"Parties may stipulate to a jury of fewer than 6, a verdict by a stated majority, or deliberation by all jurors when more than 6 were seated; absent a stipulation, and except as MCR 5.740(C) provides, a verdict in a 6-juror civil case is received when 5 jurors agree. The verdict is returned in open court, any party may require a poll, and the jury is sent back if too few agree. The court may discharge a jury for accident, consent, adjournment or mistrial, or deadlock and have a new jury drawn. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(b), which requires a unanimous verdict unless the parties stipulate otherwise, Michigan accepts a 5-of-6 verdict by default.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.515","t":"Special Verdicts","s":"The court may require a special verdict of written findings on each fact issue, but must tell the attorneys before argument and settle the form on the record or in writing, then enter judgment on the findings. A party waives jury trial on a fact issue omitted from the form unless it demands submission before the jury retires, and the court may make or is deemed to have made a finding on that issue consistent with the judgment.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.516","t":"Motion for Directed Verdict","s":"A party may move for a directed verdict at the close of the opponent’s evidence, stating specific grounds. If denied, the movant may still present evidence, and a denied motion does not waive jury trial even if all parties moved.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.517","t":"Findings by Court","s":"In a bench trial or with an advisory jury, the court must find the facts specially and state conclusions of law separately, briefly and pertinently, on the record or in a written opinion; findings are not needed on motions unless a rule requires them, and no request or exception is needed to preserve review. A party may move within 21 days after entry of judgment to amend or add findings and amend the judgment, and sufficiency of the evidence may be challenged later without a prior objection. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(b), which allows 28 days, the Michigan motion to amend findings is due within 21 days.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.518","t":"Receipt and Return or Disposal of Exhibits","s":"Trial evidence is submitted to the judge as exhibits rather than filed with the clerk, and admitted exhibits are not court records. At the end of a trial or hearing parties must retrieve their exhibits (weapons and drugs go back to the confiscating agency), and exhibits not retrieved within 56 days may be disposed of without notice. Confidential materials the court keeps must remain confidential.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.601","t":"Judgments","s":"A final judgment may grant whatever relief the winning party is entitled to, even if not demanded in the pleadings. A default judgment may not differ in kind from or exceed the amount demanded unless notice was given under MCR 2.603(B)(1).","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.602","t":"Entry of Judgments and Orders","s":"Judgments and orders must be written, signed, and dated, the signing date is the entry date, and each judgment (and any order disposing of the last claim) must state just above the judge’s signature whether it resolves the last pending claim and closes the case. An order or judgment may be signed when relief is granted, on approval of form by all parties, or under the 7-day rule: within 7 days after the ruling (or later if allowed) a party serves the proposed order with notice that it will be submitted if no written objections are filed within 7 days, and objections trigger a hearing to be held within 14 days after the first objection or as soon as practical; a party may also notice a proposed order for settlement with no motion fee. A consent order for conditional dismissal must state the reinstatement terms, and on breach the other party files an affidavit and, unless immediate entry was agreed, serves notice that judgment will enter if no verified objection is filed within 14 days (waivable in MCR 4.201 cases); the original filing date controls for limitations. The party obtaining a signed judgment or order must serve it on all parties within 7 days. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 58, Michigan sets a 7-day procedure for submitting proposed orders and requires a statement on whether the judgment closes the case.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.603","t":"Default and Default Judgment","s":"The clerk must enter a default against a party who fails to plead or defend when that failure is known or shown by a verified request, notice must go to all appearing parties and the defaulted party, and the defaulted party may not proceed until the default is set aside. Notice of a default-judgment request must be served at least 7 days before entry if the defaulted party appeared, the relief differs from or exceeds the pleading, or no specific amount was pleaded (not required after failure to appear for trial); the clerk may enter judgment for a sum certain against a nonappearing defendant who is not a minor or incompetent, if the amount does not exceed the complaint, and otherwise the party must move the court. Nonmilitary affidavits must be filed before judgment against a nonappearing defendant. A motion to set aside requires good cause and a verified statement of facts showing a meritorious defense (unless based on lack of jurisdiction), must be filed within 21 days after the default judgment if the party was personally served (subject to MCR 2.612), and relief is conditioned on paying taxable costs. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 55(c), Michigan requires a verified meritorious-defense showing and sets a 21-day limit for personally served parties.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.604","t":"Judgment in Actions Involving Multiple Claims or Multiple Parties","s":"An order resolving fewer than all claims or parties does not end the case, remains revisable until final judgment, and is not appealable as of right, though leave to appeal may be sought. Only in receivership and similar actions may the court make such an order final by expressly determining there is no just reason for delay. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 54(b), which allows partial final judgments in any case, Michigan limits that option to receivership and similar actions.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.605","t":"Declaratory Judgments","s":"In an actual controversy within its jurisdiction, a Michigan court of record may declare the rights of an interested party whether or not other relief is available, and another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief. Jury trial may be demanded as otherwise allowed, the court may expedite the hearing, declaratory judgments are reviewable as final judgments, and further relief may be granted after reasonable notice and hearing.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.610","t":"Motion for Judgment Notwithstanding the Verdict","s":"A party may move for JNOV within 21 days after entry of judgment, or within 21 days after the jury is discharged if no verdict was returned, and may join or alternatively request a new trial; a motion to set aside a verdict or for new trial is deemed to include a JNOV request. If JNOV is granted the court must also conditionally rule on any new-trial motion, and the party whose verdict was set aside must move for a new trial within 14 days after entry of judgment or waive it. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(b) and (d), which allow 28 days, Michigan sets 21 days for JNOV and 14 days for the follow-up new-trial motion.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.611","t":"New Trials; Amendment of Judgments","s":"A new trial may be granted on all or some issues for listed grounds, including irregularity, misconduct, damages influenced by passion or prejudice, a verdict grossly inadequate or excessive or against the great weight of the evidence, newly discovered evidence, errors of law or fact, or an MCR 2.612 ground; in a bench trial the court may instead take testimony or amend findings and judgment. A motion for new trial or to alter or amend a judgment must be filed and served within 21 days after entry of judgment, and the court may order a new trial on its own initiative within the same 21 days, stating the grounds. Facts outside the record must be supported by affidavits, and opposing affidavits are due within 21 days after service (extendable by stipulation for 21 more days or by the court for good cause). For damages-only error the court may deny a new trial if the nonmoving party consents in writing within 14 days to judgment at the lowest or highest amount the evidence supports (additur or remittitur), and the court must state concise reasons for its ruling. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 59, which allows 28 days for the motion and gives 14 days for opposing affidavits, Michigan sets 21 days for both.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.612","t":"Relief From Judgment or Order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time (or as MCR 7.208 allows once an appeal is pending), and a defendant subject to personal jurisdiction who did not actually know of the case may appear within 1 year after final judgment and seek relief if innocent third persons are not prejudiced. On motion the court may relieve a party from a final judgment for mistake or excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud or misconduct, a void judgment, satisfaction or a no-longer-equitable judgment, or any other justifying reason. The motion must be made within a reasonable time, and for the first three grounds within one year after entry, and it does not suspend the judgment except as MCR 2.614(A)(1) provides. Independent actions and relief for fraud on the court remain available, but old writs such as coram vobis and audita querela are abolished.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.613","t":"Limitations on Corrections of Error","s":"An error in evidence rulings or other proceedings is not a ground to grant a new trial or disturb a judgment unless refusing to do so would be inconsistent with substantial justice. Only the judge who entered a judgment or order may set it aside or stay proceedings under it, unless that judge is absent or unable to act, and trial-court fact findings may be set aside only if clearly erroneous, with regard for the trial court’s opportunity to judge credibility.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.614","t":"Stay of Proceedings to Enforce Judgment","s":"No execution or enforcement may occur until 21 days after a final judgment, and if a new-trial, reconsideration, or relief-from-judgment motion is filed and served within 21 days (or extended time allowed within that period), until 21 days after the order deciding it, though the court may enjoin property transfers meanwhile. TROs, preliminary injunctions, injunctive relief in final judgments, interlocutory receivership orders, and pre-judgment domestic relations orders on property, temporary alimony, support, or custody are enforceable immediately unless the court orders otherwise. The court may stay enforcement pending an MCR 2.612 motion or partial final judgments and may adjust injunctions pending appeal, an appeal from denial of governmental immunity automatically stays the whole case, and governmental parties need not post bond. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 62(a), which sets a 30-day automatic stay, Michigan’s automatic stay is 21 days.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.615","t":"Enforcement of Tribal Judgments","s":"Judgments, orders, subpoenas, and other judicial acts of a federally recognized tribe’s court are treated like those of a Michigan court of record, but only if the tribe has adopted a binding measure to enforce Michigan court acts and sent it to the State Court Administrative Office. Such tribal acts are presumed valid unless the objector shows lack of jurisdiction, fraud or coercion, lack of fair notice or hearing, conflict with Michigan public policy, or nonfinality. The rule does not apply where federal law requires full faith and credit.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.620","t":"Satisfaction of Judgment","s":"A judgment may be shown satisfied in whole or part by filing a signed and acknowledged satisfaction from the judgment holder or its attorney, by paying the clerk the judgment, interest, and costs on a money-only judgment, or by motion for an order of satisfaction after the court hears proofs. The clerk must note satisfaction in the court records.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.621","t":"Proceedings Supplementary to Judgment","s":"A party with a money judgment may, by motion or separate action, seek creditor’s-bill relief, supplementary relief under MCL 600.6101–600.6143, and other relief in aid of execution, with discovery available; a request to reach a debtor’s equitable interest must be verified, state the net amount due, and show equitable interests exceeding $100. Subpoenas or orders enjoining asset transfers must be served under MCR 2.105, state the amount claimed, and bear court approval, violators may be held in contempt or (if not the debtor) made liable up to the lesser of the unpaid judgment or the value transferred, and a repeat proceeding against the same party needs leave on a non-attorney affidavit. On appeal of a final supplementary order, a judgment creditor’s bond may not exceed $200, while other appellants must post a bond of at least double any money or property ordered paid or delivered, plus protections for documents and real estate.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.622","t":"Receivers","s":"For good cause the court may appoint a receiver, a fiduciary for all parties, choosing someone with sufficient competence; a nominated receiver is appointed if no objection is filed within 14 days after service of the motion (or the parties stipulate), and parties may object to a court-chosen receiver within 14 days after service of the appointing order, with the court able to shorten these times. Disqualifications include creditors and equity holders, investment bankers for the estate’s securities within 3 years, directors, officers, or employees within 2 years (unless in the estate’s best interest with no actual conflict), professionals for the estate or secured creditors within 5 years with an actual conflict, and insiders under MCL 566.31(i). The receiver must file an acceptance within 7 days after the appointment order, notify recorded interest holders within 28 days after the acceptance, and file an inventory within 35 days after the appointment order, then account and file a final report. Fee applications are deemed approved if no written objection is filed within 7 days after service of notice, receivers may not distribute funds to parties or sell real property without court order, and the court sets bond and may remove a receiver for good cause after notice and hearing.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.625","t":"Taxation of Costs","s":"Costs go to the prevailing party unless a statute or rule bars them or the court directs otherwise in writing, frivolous claims or defenses in actions filed on or after October 1, 1986 draw costs under MCL 600.2591, and the rule defines the prevailing party when there are several judgments, counts, or defendants or on circuit court review. A contract or tort plaintiff recovering less than $100 (unless reduced below $100 by a counterclaim) may recover costs no greater than the damages, setting aside a default after personal jurisdiction must be conditioned on paying taxable costs, and garnishees may recover fees and expenses in stated circumstances. For clerk taxation, the bill of costs must be presented within 28 days after the judgment is signed or after an order denying a postjudgment motion (other than under MCR 2.612(C)) or the right is waived, objections are due within 14 days after service, and court review must be sought within 7 days after notice of taxation; collection is stayed during the appeal period or a pending appeal. A settlement is deemed to include costs, Headlee Amendment plaintiffs who prevail get costs including a reasonable attorney fee, and for postjudgment costs the creditor must file a verified itemized memorandum within 28 days of a debtor’s request, the debtor may move for review within 28 days after receiving it, and disallowed amounts must be deducted within 28 days.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.626","t":"Attorney Fees","s":"An attorney fee award may include the time of a legal assistant who provided nonclerical legal support under an attorney’s supervision, if the assistant meets the criteria in Article 1, § 6 of the State Bar of Michigan bylaws.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"},{"n":"2.630","t":"Disability of Judge","s":"If, after a verdict or the filing of findings and conclusions, the trial judge cannot continue because of death, illness, or other disability, another judge of that court may perform the remaining duties. A substitute judge who is not satisfied that the duties can be performed may grant a new trial. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 63, which lets a successor judge proceed at any stage after certifying familiarity with the record, this rule addresses substitution only after a verdict or findings.","u":"https://www.courts.michigan.gov/48ec32/siteassets/rules-instructions-administrative-orders/michigan-court-rules/michigan-court-rules.pdf"}]});
